OAI_PAPER({"slug": "Nonuniqueness-for-the-periodic-hard-sphere-Boltzmann-equation-September-23-2026", "title": "Nonuniqueness for the periodic hard-sphere Boltzmann equation", "pdf": "https://github.com/openai/math/blob/main/preprints/Nonuniqueness-for-the-periodic-hard-sphere-Boltzmann-equation-September-23-2026/paper.pdf", "family": "363", "family_title": "Nonuniqueness with local conservation for the hard-sphere Boltzmann equation", "abstract_html": "We prove nonuniqueness for the periodic hard-sphere Boltzmann equation by constructing two distinct global renormalized solutions with the same nonnegative initial density on \\(\\mathbb T^3\\times\\mathbb R^3\\). This density has bounded velocity support and finite mass, energy, and absolute entropy. Both solutions conserve local mass and total momentum and satisfy the global energy and entropy-dissipation inequalities. On a common initial interval, they are strongly continuous in <i>L</i><sup>1</sup>, and their collision gains and losses are integrable.", "blocks": [{"label": "Theorem 1", "orig": "Theorem 1.1", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p>Let \\(\\mathbb T^3=\\mathbb R^3/\\mathbb Z^3\\), with volume one, and let\n\\[\n\\Omega=\\mathbb T_x^3\\times\\mathbb R_v^3,\\qquad D_t=\\partial_t+v\\cdot\\nabla_x.\n\\]\nFor \\(v,v_*\\in\\mathbb R^3\\) and \\(\\omega\\in S^2\\), define\n\\[\na=(v-v_*)\\cdot\\omega,\\qquad v'=v-a\\omega,\\qquad v_*'=v_*+a\\omega.\n\\]\nThe hard-sphere collision operator, with ordinary surface area measure on \\(S^2\\), is\n\\[\nQ(F,F)(v)=\\int_{\\mathbb R^3\\times S^2}\n|a|\\bigl(F(v')F(v_*')-F(v)F(v_*)\\bigr)\\,dv_*\\,d\\omega.\n\\]\nWrite\n\\[\nQ(F,F)=Q^+(F,F)-Q^-(F,F),\\qquad\nQ^-(F,F)=F\\nu_F,\\qquad\n\\nu_f(v)=2\\pi\\int_{\\mathbb R^3}|v-w|f(w)\\,dw.\n\\]\nThe common variables \\(t,x\\) are suppressed in collision expressions. Let\n\\[\nM(v)=(2\\pi)^{-3/2}e^{-|v|^2/2},\\qquad G=F/M,\n\\]\n\\[\n\\mathcal H_M(F)=\\int_\\Omega\n\\bigl(F\\log(F/M)-F+M\\bigr)\\,dx\\,dv,\n\\]\nand\n\\[\n\\mathcal D(F)=\\frac14\n\\int_{\\mathbb T^3\\times\\mathbb R^3\\times\\mathbb R^3\\times S^2}\n|a|(F'F_*'-FF_*)\\log\\frac{F'F_*'}{FF_*}\\,dx\\,dv\\,dv_*\\,d\\omega.\n\\]\nThe dissipation integrand takes its nonnegative lower-semicontinuous extended value at zeros, and \\(0|\\log0|=0\\).</p><p>An admissible solution means an entropy-class solution in original Definition 2.1:\n\\[\nF\\in C_w([0,\\infty);L^1(\\Omega)),\\qquad F\\ge0,\\qquad F(0)=F_0,\n\\]\nwhere weak continuity is against every bounded measurable test; on every bounded time interval,\n\\[\n\\int_\\Omega F(1+|v|^2+|\\log F|)\n\\]\nis uniformly bounded; and\n\\[\n\\frac{Q^\\pm(F,F)}{1+G}\\in L^1([0,T]\\times\\Omega)\n\\]\nfor every finite \\(T\\). For every \\(\\beta\\in C^1([0,\\infty))\\) satisfying\n\\[\n\\beta(0)=0,\\qquad |\\beta'(z)|\\le \\frac{C_\\beta}{1+z},\n\\]\nand every smooth \\(\\varphi\\), periodic in \\(x\\) and compactly supported in \\([0,\\infty)\\times\\mathbb R_v^3\\),\n\\[\n\\int_0^\\infty\\!\\!\\int_\\Omega\n\\left[M\\beta(G)(\\partial_t\\varphi+v\\cdot\\nabla_x\\varphi)\n+\\beta'(G)Q(F,F)\\varphi\\right]\n+\\int_\\Omega M\\beta(F_0/M)\\varphi(0)=0.\n\\]\nMoreover, with\n\\[\n\\rho=\\int F\\,dv,\\qquad j=\\int vF\\,dv,\n\\]\nthe solution satisfies \\(\\partial_t\\rho+\\nabla_x\\cdot j=0\\) with the prescribed initial density, conserves total momentum, and for every \\(t\\ge0\\),\n\\[\n\\int_\\Omega |v|^2F(t)\\le\\int_\\Omega |v|^2F_0,\n\\]\n\\[\n\\mathcal H_M(F(t))+\\int_0^t\\mathcal D(F(s))\\,ds\\le\\mathcal H_M(F_0).\n\\]\nLocal momentum and local energy conservation are not additional admissibility requirements.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There is a nonnegative density \\(F_0\\) on \\(\\mathbb T^3\\times\\mathbb R^3\\), with bounded velocity support and\n\\[\n\\int_\\Omega F_0(1+|v|^2+|\\log F_0|)\\,dx\\,dv&lt;\\infty,\n\\]\nfor which\n\\[\nD_tF=Q(F,F),\\qquad F(0)=F_0,\n\\]\nhas two distinct global admissible solutions. On some common initial interval both solutions belong to \\(C([0,T];L^1(\\Omega))\\), and their separate collision gains and losses belong to \\(L^1([0,T]\\times\\Omega)\\). Thus both obey the unrenormalized equation on the interval where their difference is established.</p>", "proof": "<p>Use the datum of Proposition 2. Its finite mass, energy, absolute entropy, and bounded velocity support have been proved. Proposition 8 fixes the remaining construction parameters, after which the final small geometric, thermal, and time parameters are chosen. Fix a dormant solution \\(F^d\\) on \\([0,T_0]\\) supplied by Proposition 5.</p><p>For every \\(0&lt;T\\le T_0\\), Proposition 4 gives cap approximations and Proposition 5 gives at least one admissible cap limit with exactly the same datum.</p><p>Suppose there are horizons \\(T_k\\downarrow0\\) at which a cap limit can equal \\(F^d\\) on \\([0,T_k]\\). For each fixed horizon choose approximations converging to this particular common limit. Using a countable exhaustion by nonnegative smooth compact tests of the region in Proposition 9, choose indices \\(N_k\\) diagonally so that\n\\[\ns_{N_k}/T_k\\to0,\\qquad\nf_k(t,x,v):=T_k^{-1}F_k(T_kt,T_kx,v)\\to0\n\\]\ndistributionally on that region. For each fixed \\(T_k\\), a rescaled test is a smooth physical test with finite, possibly large, norm. Weak convergence permits its error to be made arbitrarily small while simultaneously making \\(s_{N_k}/T_k\\) small. No uniform rate in \\(T_k\\) is required.</p><p>Write \\(F_k=c_k+h_k\\), choose a cap event, and set\n\\[\nr_k=t_{*,k}+s_{N_k}.\n\\]\nProposition 6 gives, after subselection,\n\\[\ns_{N_k}/r_k\\to l,\\qquad T_k/r_k\\to U\\in[1,\\infty],\n\\]\nand a dilated hot limit \\(g\\) on \\((0,U)\\). Proposition 7 gives \\(g\\not\\equiv0\\), while Proposition 8 gives \\(U&lt;\\infty\\). Since \\(g\\) is a density and the axis is null, there are\n\\[\n0\\le\\psi\\in C_c^\\infty((0,U)\\times\\mathbb R_x^3\\times\\{\\bar v\\ne0\\}),\n\\qquad\\psi\\le1,\\qquad\\delta&gt;0,\n\\]\nsuch that\n\\[\n\\int\\psi g&gt;2\\delta,\\qquad\n\\int\\psi(t,x,v)r_k^{-1}h_k(r_kt,r_kx,v)\\,dt\\,dx\\,dv\\ge\\delta\n\\]\nfor all large \\(k\\).</p><p>Put\n\\[\n\\rho_k=r_k/T_k\\to\\rho=1/U&gt;0.\n\\]\nPositivity and \\(F_k\\ge h_k\\) give\n\\[\n0\\le r_k^{-1}h_k(r_kt,r_kx,v)\n\\le\\rho_k^{-1}f_k(\\rho_kt,\\rho_kx,v).\n\\]\nThe image of any compact subset of\n\\[\n(0,U)\\times\\mathbb R_x^3\\times\\{\\bar v\\ne0\\}\n\\]\nlies, for all large \\(k\\), in one compact subset of the region where \\(f_k\\to0\\). A single smooth nonnegative test equal to one on the larger compact set dominates the changing tests. Distributional convergence and positivity therefore make the right side tend to zero locally in \\(L^1\\). Thus \\(g=0\\) off the axis. Since \\(g\\le(t+l)H\\) is a density, it also vanishes almost everywhere on the axis. This contradicts Proposition 7.</p><p>Consequently, for all sufficiently small horizons no cap limit equals the fixed dormant limit. Choose one such horizon and one cap limit. The two admissible initial-interval solutions have the same datum and differ on a set of positive spacetime-velocity measure. Proposition 5 continues both globally while preserving all admissibility conditions. Their initial-interval strong \\(L^1\\) continuity and separate collision integrability are also supplied by that proposition, completing the theorem.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 1", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Collision geometry and uniform velocity-kernel estimates.", "statement": "<p>Collision geometry and uniform velocity-kernel estimates.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p>Use the collision normalization of Theorem 1. Write\n\\[\nv=(\\bar v,v_3)\\in\\mathbb R^2\\times\\mathbb R,\\qquad\n\\langle v\\rangle=(1+|v|^2)^{1/2}.\n\\]\nFix \\(1&lt;q&lt;2\\), \\(m=100\\), and define\n\\[\nW(v)=1+|\\bar v|^{-q},\\qquad\nH(v)=e^{-|v|^2}\\langle v\\rangle^{-m}W(v).\n\\]\nValues on the velocity axis are irrelevant to essential estimates. Set\n\\[\n\\|f\\|_H=\\operatorname*{ess\\,sup}_{x,v}\\frac{|f(x,v)|}{H(v)}.\n\\]\nLet \\(P(f,g)\\) denote the symmetric bilinear gain, with \\(P(F,F)=Q^+(F,F)\\). A superscript \\(b\\) means that the common invariant cutoff\n\\[\n\\mathbf1_{\\{|v-v_*|&lt;b\\}}\n\\]\nis inserted into gain and loss.</p><p>For \\(d&gt;0\\), let\n\\[\n\\mathcal G_d(d\\bar p)=\\frac{1}{\\pi d^2}e^{-|\\bar p|^2/d^2}\\,d\\bar p,\n\\qquad \\mathcal G_0=\\delta_0.\n\\]\nOriginal Definition 3.3 defines \\(\\mathcal S_L\\), \\(L\\ge1\\), as the probability mixtures of product probability measures\n\\[\nc(dp)=\\mathcal G_d(d\\bar p)\\rho(dp_3),\\qquad\n0\\le d\\le\\frac18,\\qquad\n\\int_\\mathbb R e^{8a^2}\\rho(da)\\le L.\n\\]\nDomination with amplitude \\(A_0\\) means domination as measures by \\(A_0\\) times a member of \\(\\mathcal S_L\\). Define the complete normalized kernel\n\\[\n\\mathcal K_c(v;dp\\,d\\xi)\n=2c(dp)\\delta\\bigl((v-p)\\cdot(\\xi-v)\\bigr)\\frac{H(\\xi)}{H(v)}\\,d\\xi.\n\\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The Carleman representation, uniform sheet gain and tail bounds, axial nonconcentration estimates, Gaussian-parameter bounds, and quadratic estimate established in Lemmas 1.1–1.6 hold. In particular,\n\\[\nP(c,H)\\le C_{q,m,L}H\\quad(c\\in\\mathcal S_L),\n\\qquad\nP(H,H)\\le C_{q,m}H.\n\\]\nAll upper estimates remain valid with the collision cutoff. The function \\(H\\) has every polynomial velocity moment and belongs to \\(L^p(\\mathbb R^3)\\) for \\(1\\le p&lt;2/q\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Lemmas 1.1–1.6 establish the asserted collision and normalized-kernel estimates. The cutoff gain is bounded by the uncut gain. The local singularity of \\(H\\) is \\(|\\bar v|^{-q}\\) in two dimensions; its Gaussian decay gives every polynomial moment and \\(H\\in L^p\\) for \\(1\\le p&lt;2/q\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 1.1", "orig": "Lemma 3.1, Collision geometry", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Under Proposition 1's collision normalization, \\( P(c,h)(v)=2\\int_{\\mathbb R^3}c(p)\\,dp \\int_{\\mathbb R^3}\\delta\\bigl((v-p)\\cdot(\\xi-v)\\bigr)h(\\xi)\\,d\\xi. \\) For \\(p\\ne v\\), the inner delta is surface measure on …", "statement": "<p>Under Proposition 1's collision normalization,\n\\[\nP(c,h)(v)=2\\int_{\\mathbb R^3}c(p)\\,dp\n\\int_{\\mathbb R^3}\\delta\\bigl((v-p)\\cdot(\\xi-v)\\bigr)h(\\xi)\\,d\\xi.\n\\]\nFor \\(p\\ne v\\), the inner delta is surface measure on\n\\[\n(v-p)\\cdot(\\xi-v)=0\n\\]\ndivided by \\(|v-p|\\). For incoming velocities \\(p,\\xi\\), the outgoing measure has total mass \\(2\\pi|p-\\xi|\\) and is uniform on the sphere with diameter \\([p,\\xi]\\). The formula also defines the gain for a nonnegative velocity measure \\(c\\) and a nonnegative density \\(h\\), whenever the integral is finite almost everywhere.</p>", "proof": "<p>Put \\(g=p-\\xi\\). Reflection sends \\(g\\) to \\(g-2(g\\cdot\\omega)\\omega\\). On either hemisphere, the change from the polar angle of \\(\\omega\\) to the reflected direction pushes \\(|g\\cdot\\omega|\\,d\\omega\\) to \\((|g|/4)\\,d\\sigma\\). The two hemispheres give\n\\[\n\\frac{|p-\\xi|}{2}\\int_{S^2}\n\\psi\\left(\\frac{p+\\xi}{2}+\\frac{|p-\\xi|}{2}\\sigma\\right)\\,d\\sigma.\n\\]\nIts mass is \\(2\\pi|p-\\xi|\\). The affine map multiplies surface area by \\(|p-\\xi|^2/4\\), so the density on the image sphere is \\(2/|p-\\xi|\\).</p><p>That sphere is the zero set of\n\\[\nf(v)=(v-p)\\cdot(\\xi-v),\n\\]\nand \\(|\\nabla_vf|=|p-\\xi|\\) on it. Coarea identifies the outgoing measure with \\(2\\delta(f(v))\\,dv\\). Integrating against the incoming densities and using Tonelli gives the asserted formula. The usual pre/post-collisional substitution identifies it with the gain. Coincident incoming velocities have zero rate. The same identities can first be integrated against \\(c(dp)\\), proving the measure extension and symmetry.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 1.2", "orig": "Lemma 3.2, Transverse averaging and axial slices", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Let \\(\\lambda\\) be a probability mixture of uniform probabilities \\(\\mathcal U_d\\) on the disks \\(\\{|\\bar p|<d\\}\\), \\(d>0\\), and \\(\\delta_0\\).", "statement": "<p>Let \\(\\lambda\\) be a probability mixture of uniform probabilities \\(\\mathcal U_d\\) on the disks \\(\\{|\\bar p|&lt;d\\}\\), \\(d&gt;0\\), and \\(\\delta_0\\). Let \\(\\rho\\) be any axial probability measure, without assumptions on its density or support, and put \\(c=\\lambda\\otimes\\rho\\). For \\(R\\ge1\\), \\(\\bar v\\ne0\\), and an interval \\(I\\) of length \\(0&lt;\\ell\\le2R\\),\n\\[\n\\frac1{W(v)}\\int c(dp)\n\\int_{|\\xi-v|\\le R}\\delta\\bigl((v-p)\\cdot(\\xi-v)\\bigr)W(\\xi)\\,d\\xi\n\\le C_qR^2,\n\\]\n\\[\n\\frac1{W(v)}\\int c(dp)\n\\int_{\\substack{|\\xi-v|\\le R\\\\ \\xi_3\\in I}}\n\\delta\\bigl((v-p)\\cdot(\\xi-v)\\bigr)W(\\xi)\\,d\\xi\n\\le C_q\\bigl(R\\ell+R^{q-1}\\ell^{2-q}\\bigr).\n\\]\nThe constants are independent of all transverse widths and of \\(\\rho\\). In particular, these estimates hold for \\(\\lambda=\\mathcal G_d\\), \\(d\\ge0\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Set\n\\[\nr=|\\bar v|,\\qquad n=\\bar v-\\bar p,\\qquad e_n=n/|n|.\n\\]\nExcept on a transverse null set, \\(n\\ne0\\). For fixed \\(p_3,\\xi_3\\), the transverse delta is line measure divided by \\(|n|\\), on a line whose signed distance from zero is\n\\[\nA-B(\\xi_3-v_3),\\qquad A=e_n\\cdot\\bar v,\\qquad B=\\frac{v_3-p_3}{|n|}.\n\\]\nThe regular part of its integral is at most \\(2R/|n|\\). Its singular part is bounded by\n\\[\n\\frac1{|n|}\\int_\\mathbb R\n\\bigl((A-B(\\xi_3-v_3))^2+s^2\\bigr)^{-q/2}\\,ds\n=\\frac{a_q}{|n|}|A-B(\\xi_3-v_3)|^{1-q},\n\\]\nwhere\n\\[\na_q=\\int_\\mathbb R(1+s^2)^{-q/2}\\,ds&lt;\\infty.\n\\]\nInfinite values on a zero-distance slice are harmless in the nonnegative iterated integral.</p><p>For every interval \\(J\\subset[-R,R]\\) of length at most \\(\\ell\\),\n\\[\n\\int_J|A-Bx|^{1-q}\\,dx\n\\le C_qR^{q-1}\\ell^{2-q}|A|^{1-q}.\n\\]\nIf \\(2R|B|\\le|A|\\), the integrand is bounded by \\(C_q|A|^{1-q}\\). Otherwise, integration about the possible root gives\n\\[\nC_q|B|^{1-q}\\ell^{2-q}\n\\le C_qR^{q-1}|A|^{1-q}\\ell^{2-q}.\n\\]</p><p>Next,\n\\[\n\\int\\frac{1+|e_n\\cdot e_{\\bar v}|^{1-q}}{|n|}\\,\\lambda(d\\bar p)\n\\le\\frac{C_q}{r}.\n\\]\nFor a disk of radius \\(d\\le r/2\\), use \\(|n|\\ge r/2\\) and \\(e_n\\cdot e_{\\bar v}\\ge1/3\\). For \\(d&gt;r/2\\), use polar coordinates for \\(n\\), whose range lies in \\(|n|&lt;3d\\). The factor \\(|n|^{-1}\\) cancels the radial Jacobian, and\n\\[\n\\int_0^{2\\pi}(1+|\\cos\\theta|^{1-q})\\,d\\theta&lt;\\infty\n\\]\nbecause \\(q&lt;2\\). Division by the disk area gives \\(C_q/d\\le2C_q/r\\). The atom at zero satisfies the same estimate. The Gaussian is a probability mixture of disks with mixture density\n\\[\n\\frac{2a^3}{d^4}e^{-a^2/d^2}\\,da.\n\\]</p><p>Combining these estimates bounds the interval integral by\n\\[\nC_qR\\ell\\frac{r^{-1}}{1+r^{-q}}\n+C_qR^{q-1}\\ell^{2-q}\\frac{r^{-q}}{1+r^{-q}}.\n\\]\nBoth fractions are bounded. The estimates are independent of \\(p_3\\), so integration against \\(\\rho\\) preserves them. Taking the full axial range of length \\(2R\\) gives the first estimate.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 1.3", "orig": "Lemma 3.4, Uniform sheet gain bound", "depth": 1, "snippet": "For every \\(L<\\infty\\), \\( P(c,H)(v)\\le C_{q,m,L}H(v)\\qquad\\text{for almost every }v,\\quad c\\in\\mathcal S_L. \\) More quantitatively, for \\(R\\ge1\\) …", "statement": "<p>For every \\(L&lt;\\infty\\),\n\\[\nP(c,H)(v)\\le C_{q,m,L}H(v)\\qquad\\text{for almost every }v,\\quad c\\in\\mathcal S_L.\n\\]\nMore quantitatively, for \\(R\\ge1\\),\n\\[\n\\mathcal K_c\\bigl(v;R&lt;|p|+|\\xi-v|\\le2R\\bigr)\n\\le C_{q,m,L}R^2e^{-cR^2},\n\\]\nwhere \\(c&gt;0\\) is absolute. Under sheet domination with amplitude \\(A_0\\), multiply these bounds by \\(A_0\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>By positivity, treat an elementary sheet. Put \\(k=\\xi-v\\), \\(w=p+k\\). On the collision plane,\n\\[\n|p|^2+|\\xi|^2=|v|^2+|w|^2,\n\\]\n\\[\n\\frac{H(\\xi)}{H(v)}\n=e^{|p|^2-|w|^2}\\frac{\\langle v\\rangle^m}{\\langle\\xi\\rangle^m}\n\\frac{W(\\xi)}{W(v)}.\n\\]\nThe same identity gives \\(\\langle v\\rangle\\le\\langle p\\rangle\\langle\\xi\\rangle\\). Since\n\\[\n\\langle p\\rangle^m\\le C_me^{|p|^2},\n\\]\nthe kernel is bounded by\n\\[\nC_me^{2|p|^2}c(dp)e^{-|w|^2}\n\\delta((v-p)\\cdot k)\\frac{W(v+k)}{W(v)}\\,dk.\n\\]\nFor \\(0&lt;d\\le1/8\\),\n\\[\ne^{2|\\bar p|^2}\\mathcal G_d(d\\bar p)\n\\le4e^{-46|\\bar p|^2}\\mathcal G_{2d}(d\\bar p).\n\\]\nIndeed, the ratio is\n\\[\n4\\exp\\left[-\\left(\\frac3{4d^2}-2\\right)|\\bar p|^2\\right],\n\\]\nand \\(3/(4d^2)-2\\ge46\\). The \\(d=0\\) interpretation is immediate. The tilted axial measure\n\\[\n\\widetilde\\rho(da)=e^{2a^2}\\rho(da)\n\\]\nhas mass at most \\(L\\), with\n\\[\n\\widetilde\\rho\\{|a|&gt;u\\}\\le Le^{-6u^2}.\n\\]\nThus \\(e^{2|p|^2}c(dp)\\) is bounded by \\(4L\\) times a product of a centered transverse Gaussian and an axial probability. Its restriction to \\(|p|&gt;u\\) is bounded by \\(CLe^{-cu^2}\\) times a sum of at most two such product probabilities, by splitting into transverse and axial tails. Their normalized axial laws need no support bound, by Lemma 1.2.</p><p>On the stated shell, either \\(|p|&gt;R/4\\) or \\(|w|&gt;R/2\\), since \\(|p|+|k|\\le2|p|+|w|\\). The first case supplies the tilted-measure tail \\(CLe^{-cR^2}\\); the second supplies \\(e^{-|w|^2}\\le e^{-R^2/4}\\). The remaining integral has \\(|k|\\le2R\\), so Lemma 1.2 bounds it by \\(C_qR^2\\). The base region is bounded in the same way. Summing dyadic shells proves the gain bound and finiteness of the normalized kernel.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 1.4", "orig": "Lemma 3.5, Uniform tightness and axis estimates", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Uniformly for \\(c\\in\\mathcal S_L\\), outgoing velocities off the axis, and bounded sheet amplitudes, the following hold:", "statement": "<p>Uniformly for \\(c\\in\\mathcal S_L\\), outgoing velocities off the axis, and bounded sheet amplitudes, the following hold:\n\\[\n\\lim_{R\\to\\infty}\\sup_c\\operatorname*{ess\\,sup}_v\n\\mathcal K_c(v;|p|+|\\xi-v|&gt;R)=0,\n\\]\n\\[\n\\lim_{M\\to\\infty}\\sup_c\\operatorname*{ess\\,sup}_{|v|&gt;M}\n\\mathcal K_c(v;\\mathbb R^3\\times\\mathbb R^3)=0.\n\\]\nFor fixed \\(M,\\delta&gt;0\\),\n\\[\n\\lim_{\\varepsilon\\downarrow0}\\sup_c\n\\operatorname*{ess\\,sup}_{\\substack{|v|\\le M\\\\0&lt;|\\bar v|\\le\\varepsilon}}\n\\mathcal K_c(v;|\\bar\\xi|\\ge\\delta)=0,\n\\]\n\\[\n\\lim_{\\varepsilon\\downarrow0}\\sup_c\n\\operatorname*{ess\\,sup}_{\\substack{|v|\\le M\\\\0&lt;|\\bar v|\\le\\varepsilon}}\n\\mathcal K_c(v;|v_3-p_3|\\ge\\delta)=0.\n\\]\nFor \\(R\\ge1\\) and every interval \\(I\\) of length \\(0&lt;\\ell\\le1\\),\n\\[\n\\mathcal K_c(v;|p|+|\\xi-v|\\le R,\\ \\xi_3\\in I)\n\\le C_{q,m,L,R}(\\ell+\\ell^{2-q}),\n\\]\nand consequently\n\\[\n\\lim_{\\ell\\downarrow0}\\sup_c\\operatorname*{ess\\,sup}_v\n\\sup_{|I|\\le\\ell}\\mathcal K_c(v;\\xi_3\\in I)=0.\n\\]\nIf \\(\\mathcal S_L(\\varepsilon)\\) restricts all elementary transverse widths to at most \\(\\varepsilon\\), then for \\(M&lt;\\infty\\), \\(r_0&gt;0\\),\n\\[\n\\lim_{\\delta\\downarrow0}\\limsup_{\\varepsilon\\downarrow0}\n\\sup_{c\\in\\mathcal S_L(\\varepsilon)}\n\\operatorname*{ess\\,sup}_{\\substack{|v|\\le M\\\\|\\bar v|\\ge r_0}}\n\\mathcal K_c(v;|\\bar\\xi|&lt;\\delta)=0.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Lemma 1.3 removes the tails in \\(|p|+|k|\\). Work on \\(|p|+|k|\\le R\\), where the exponential and polynomial factors in the preceding proof are bounded by constants depending on \\(R\\).</p><p>If \\(|\\bar v|&gt;2R+1\\), then \\(|\\bar v-\\bar p|\\ge|\\bar v|/2\\), and the transverse line estimate gives \\(C_R/|\\bar v|\\). If \\(|v_3|&gt;2R+1\\), integrate the delta in \\(\\xi_3\\), with divisor at least \\(|v_3|/2\\), and use\n\\[\n\\sup_{a\\in\\mathbb R^2}\\int_{|\\zeta-a|\\le R}(1+|\\zeta|^{-q})\\,d\\zeta\n\\le C_q(R^2+R^{2-q}).\n\\]\nFor \\(|a|\\le2R\\), enlarge to the disk of radius \\(3R\\); otherwise \\(|\\zeta|&gt;|a|/2&gt;R\\). This proves the large-output estimate.</p><p>On \\(|\\bar\\xi|\\ge\\delta\\), \\(W(\\xi)\\le1+\\delta^{-q}\\), so the regular line estimate and Lemma 1.2's transverse averaging yield\n\\[\nC_{R,\\delta}\\frac{|\\bar v|^{-1}}{1+|\\bar v|^{-q}}\n\\le C_{R,\\delta}|\\bar v|^{q-1}.\n\\]\nOn \\(|v_3-p_3|\\ge\\delta\\), integration in \\(\\xi_3\\) gives\n\\[\nC_{R,\\delta}/W(v)\\le C_{R,\\delta}|\\bar v|^q.\n\\]\nThe axial interval estimate follows from Lemma 1.2. Removing tails gives its global non-atomic consequence.</p><p>For the last assertion, first remove \\(|\\bar p|&gt;r_0/4\\), using\n\\[\n\\mathbf1_{\\{|\\bar p|&gt;r_0/4\\}}\\mathcal G_d(d\\bar p)\n\\le2e^{-r_0^2/(32d^2)}\\mathcal G_{\\sqrt2d}(d\\bar p)\n\\]\nand Lemma 1.3. On the retained region, with the notation of Lemma 1.2,\n\\[\n|n|\\ge\\frac34r_0,\\qquad\nA=\\frac{(\\bar v-\\bar p)\\cdot\\bar v}{|\\bar v-\\bar p|}\n\\ge\\frac35r_0.\n\\]\nIf \\(|v_3-p_3|\\le r_0^2/(8R)\\), then\n\\[\n|B(\\xi_3-v_3)|\\le r_0/6,\n\\]\nso the collision line cannot meet \\(|\\bar\\xi|&lt;\\delta\\) when \\(\\delta&lt;r_0/3\\). Otherwise integrate first in \\(\\xi_3\\), obtaining\n\\[\nC_{R,r_0}\\int_{|\\bar\\xi|&lt;\\delta}(1+|\\bar\\xi|^{-q})\\,d\\bar\\xi\n\\le C_{R,r_0}(\\delta^2+\\delta^{2-q}).\n\\]\nLet the widths tend to zero, then \\(\\delta\\) tend to zero, and finally remove the truncation.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 1.5", "orig": "Lemma 3.6, Gaussian parameter tails", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Let \\(w=(\\bar w,w_3)\\) have a nonnegative measure density \\( j(w)\\le C_0\\pi^{-3/2}e^{-|w|^2}. \\) Suppose its velocity image is …", "statement": "<p>Let \\(w=(\\bar w,w_3)\\) have a nonnegative measure density\n\\[\nj(w)\\le C_0\\pi^{-3/2}e^{-|w|^2}.\n\\]\nSuppose its velocity image is\n\\[\n\\bar p=d\\bar w,\\qquad p_3=\\Phi(w_3),\\qquad\n0\\le d\\le\\frac1{16},\\qquad\n|\\Phi(a)|\\le A+\\delta|a|,\\qquad0\\le\\delta\\le\\frac1{16},\n\\]\nwhere \\(\\Phi\\) is measurable. The image is dominated by \\(C_0\\) times a sheet in \\(\\mathcal S_{L_A}\\), with \\(L_A\\) depending only on \\(A\\). If \\(c_{&gt;B}\\) is the image after imposing \\(|w|&gt;B\\), then\n\\[\n\\operatorname*{ess\\,sup}_v\\frac{P(c_{&gt;B},H)(v)}{H(v)}\n\\le C_{q,m,A,C_0}e^{-B^2/2}.\n\\]\nThe assertions persist under probability mixtures, under positive mixtures of total mass \\(B_0\\) with the bounds multiplied by \\(B_0\\), and under measurable masks in \\([0,1]\\).</p><p>In particular, if\n\\[\np_3=a(z-tp_3)+dw_3,\\qquad u=a(z-tu),\n\\]\nwhere \\(a\\) is nondecreasing, then\n\\[\n|p_3-u|\\le d|w_3|,\n\\]\nso the lemma applies when \\(|u|\\le A\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>The Gaussian majorant is a product. Its transverse image is \\(\\mathcal G_d\\); its axial image is\n\\[\n\\rho=\\Phi_*(\\pi^{-1/2}e^{-a^2}\\,da).\n\\]\nNo differentiation or inversion of \\(\\Phi\\) is needed. Since\n\\[\ne^{8|\\Phi(a)|^2}\\le e^{16A^2}e^{16\\delta^2a^2},\n\\]\neven the wider axial Gaussian gives\n\\[\ne^{16A^2}(1-32\\delta^2)^{-1/2}\\le2e^{16A^2}.\n\\]\nTake \\(L_A=2e^{16A^2}\\).</p><p>On the parameter tail,\n\\[\n\\mathbf1_{\\{|w|&gt;B\\}}e^{-|w|^2}\n\\le e^{-B^2/2}e^{-|w|^2/2}.\n\\]\nAfter normalization this is \\(2^{3/2}e^{-B^2/2}\\) times a Gaussian probability with widths enlarged by \\(\\sqrt2\\). Its transverse image width is at most \\(\\sqrt2/16&lt;1/8\\), and its axial image has the preceding exponential moment. Lemma 1.3 gives the estimate. Normalize a mixing measure of positive mass \\(B_0\\), retain \\(B_0\\) outside the sheet, and apply the same argument componentwise before mixing. Masks only decrease the measures.</p><p>Finally, \\(p\\mapsto p-a(z-tp)\\) is increasing with inverse Lipschitz constant at most one; its values at \\(p_3\\) and \\(u\\) differ by \\(dw_3\\). Fixed positive Gaussian exponents other than one are handled by a fixed parameter rescaling and corresponding smaller physical widths.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 1.6", "orig": "Lemma 3.7, Quadratic gain bound", "depth": 1, "snippet": "For the weight in Proposition 1, \\( P(H,H)\\le C_{q,m}H\\qquad\\text{almost everywhere}. \\) The proof works for every \\(m>5\\).", "statement": "<p>For the weight in Proposition 1,\n\\[\nP(H,H)\\le C_{q,m}H\\qquad\\text{almost everywhere}.\n\\]\nThe proof works for every \\(m&gt;5\\).</p><p>Original Remark 3.8 records that a single normalized gain need not vanish at an axial endpoint.</p>", "proof": "<p>By symmetry in the incoming velocities and\n\\[\n|v|^2\\le|p|^2+|\\xi|^2,\n\\]\nit suffices, at a factor two, to retain \\(|\\xi|\\ge|v|/2\\). The collision identity gives\n\\[\n\\frac{P(H,H)(v)}{H(v)}\n\\le C_m\\int_{|\\xi|\\ge|v|/2}\n\\delta((v-p)\\cdot(\\xi-v))\ne^{-|p+\\xi-v|^2}\\langle p\\rangle^{-m}\n\\frac{W(p)W(\\xi)}{W(v)}\\,dp\\,d\\xi.\n\\]\nPut \\(k=\\xi-v\\), \\(w=p+k\\). On\n\\[\nR&lt;|p|+|k|\\le2R,\\qquad R\\ge1,\n\\]\neither \\(|p|&gt;R/4\\) or \\(|w|&gt;R/2\\); hence\n\\[\n\\langle p\\rangle^{-m}e^{-|w|^2}\n\\le C_mR^{-m}e^{-|w|^2/2}.\n\\]\nThe measure \\(W(p)\\mathbf1_{\\{|p|\\le2R\\}}\\,dp\\) is dominated by\n\\[\n(1+|\\bar p|^{-q})\\mathbf1_{\\{|\\bar p|\\le2R\\}}\\,d\\bar p\n\\otimes\\mathbf1_{\\{|p_3|\\le2R\\}}\\,dp_3.\n\\]\nIts transverse factor is a positive disk mixture, since\n\\[\n|\\bar p|^{-q}\\mathbf1_{\\{|\\bar p|\\le D\\}}\n=D^{-q}\\mathbf1_{\\{|\\bar p|\\le D\\}}\n+q\\int_0^D a^{-q-1}\\mathbf1_{\\{|\\bar p|\\le a\\}}\\,da.\n\\]\nAfter disk normalization, the transverse mass is\n\\[\n\\pi D^2+\\frac{2\\pi D^{2-q}}{2-q}.\n\\]\nFor \\(D=2R\\), the product mixture has mass at most \\(C_qR^3\\). Discard the remaining Gaussian and the incoming-speed restriction, and apply Lemma 1.2 to obtain the shell bound \\(C_{q,m}R^{5-m}\\). The base region is bounded similarly. Dyadic summation proves the result.</p><p>For the axial endpoint in original Remark 3.8, take \\(c=\\delta_{(0,0,u)}\\), \\(v_\\varepsilon=(\\varepsilon,0,u)\\). The collision plane is \\(\\xi_1=\\varepsilon\\), and\n\\[\n\\frac{P(c,H)(v_\\varepsilon)}{H(v_\\varepsilon)}\n=\\frac2{\\varepsilon H(v_\\varepsilon)}\n\\int_{\\mathbb R^2}H(\\varepsilon,y,z)\\,dy\\,dz.\n\\]\nThe regular part is \\(O(\\varepsilon^{q-1})\\). In the singular part set \\(y=\\varepsilon s\\). Dominated convergence gives\n\\[\n\\lim_{\\varepsilon\\downarrow0}\n\\frac{P(c,H)(v_\\varepsilon)}{H(v_\\varepsilon)}\n=2a_qe^{u^2}\\langle u\\rangle^m\n\\int_\\mathbb R e^{-z^2}\\langle z\\rangle^{-m}\\,dz&gt;0.\n\\]\nThe corresponding axial marginal converges to\n\\[\n2a_qe^{u^2}\\langle u\\rangle^m e^{-z^2}\\langle z\\rangle^{-m}\\,dz.\n\\]\nThus transverse concentration is compatible with uniform axial non-atomicity, motivating two gains in the later axial argument.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 2", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Cold-jet geometry and the common datum.", "statement": "<p>Cold-jet geometry and the common datum.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p>Fix\n\\[\nK\\ge1,\\qquad0&lt;\\eta\\le\\log2,\\qquad0&lt;\\alpha\\le\\frac14,\\qquad0&lt;\\gamma\\le1.\n\\]\nSet\n\\[\n\\bar s=\\frac18,\\qquad\\bar T=\\frac1{32},\\qquad\n0&lt;s_0\\le\\bar s,\\qquad0&lt;T_0\\le\\bar T,\n\\]\n\\[\ns_j=s_0e^{-j\\eta},\\quad p_j=s_j^2,\\quad\nc_\\eta=\\frac{e^\\eta-1}{16},\\quad\n\\delta_j=\\frac{c_\\eta}{4}s_j^2.\n\\]\nFor \\(y\\in\\mathbb R^2\\), define\n\\[\nA_j=\\{s_j+c_\\eta p_j&lt;|y|&lt;e^\\eta s_j-c_\\eta p_j\\},\n\\]\n\\[\nP_j^+=\\bigcup_{k\\in\\mathbb Z}\\{kp_j&lt;y_1&lt;(k+\\tfrac14)p_j\\},\n\\]\n\\[\nP_j^-=\\bigcup_{k\\in\\mathbb Z}\n\\{(k+\\tfrac12)p_j&lt;y_1&lt;(k+\\tfrac34)p_j\\},\n\\qquad E_j^\\sigma=A_j\\cap P_j^\\sigma.\n\\]\nEach stripe pattern has fraction \\(\\theta=1/4\\); this fraction is not asserted to hold exactly after intersection with the annulus. Set\n\\[\n\\widehat E_j^\\sigma=\\{y:\\operatorname{dist}(y,E_j^\\sigma)\\le\\delta_j\\},\n\\qquad a=\\frac18,\\qquad b_{\\rm sp}=\\frac1{16}.\n\\]\nFor \\(i=(j,\\sigma,n)\\), \\(n=(\\bar n,n_3)\\in\\mathbb Z^3\\), put\n\\[\n\\mathcal O_i(t)\n=(\\widehat E_j^\\sigma+\\bar n)\n\\times[n_3-a-2t-b_{\\rm sp},\\,n_3+a+2t+b_{\\rm sp}].\n\\]\nFix a nonnegative smooth probability density \\(J\\), supported in the unit ball of \\(\\mathbb R^3\\), and define\n\\[\nS(\\ell)=\\operatorname{sech}^2\\ell,\\qquad\na_s^\\sigma(z)=\\sigma+\\alpha\\tanh(\\gamma z/s),\\qquad\nd_s=d_0s^{10}.\n\\]\nChoose \\(0&lt;d_0\\le1\\) sufficiently small that\n\\[\nd_0\\bar s^{10}\\le\\frac1{64},\\qquad\n\\bar T d_0\\bar s^8\\le c_\\eta/8.\n\\]\nOn the periodic lift, set\n\\[\nc_i^0(x,v)=\n\\mathbf1_{E_j^\\sigma}(y-\\bar n)\\mathbf1_{(-a,a)}(z-n_3)\n\\frac K{s_j}S\\!\\left(\\frac{\\gamma(z-n_3)}{s_j}\\right)\nd_{s_j}^{-3}\nJ\\!\\left(\\frac{v-a_{s_j}^\\sigma(z-n_3)e_3}{d_{s_j}}\\right),\n\\]\n\\[\nF_0=\\sum_{j\\ge1}\\sum_{\\sigma=\\pm1}\\sum_{n\\in\\mathbb Z^3}c_{(j,\\sigma,n)}^0.\n\\]\nLet \\(F_0^{(N)}\\) retain \\(j\\le N\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The spatial enlargements are pairwise disjoint on \\([0,\\bar T]\\). The periodic density \\(F_0\\) has velocity support in \\(\\{|v|&lt;2\\}\\) and finite mass, energy, and absolute entropy. Its truncations converge in mass, energy, and relative entropy.</p><p>For every fixed nonnegative bounded smooth compactly supported \\(\\chi(x,v)\\), periodized after replacing \\(x\\) by \\(x/s_N\\), and every \\(0\\le\\lambda_N\\le L_0&lt;\\infty\\), the densities\n\\[\nF_0^{(N)}+\\lambda_Ns_N\\chi(x/s_N,v)\n\\]\nhave the same limiting mass, energy, and relative entropy as \\(F_0\\). The parameter \\(s_0\\) may be decreased to separate the initial periodic seed copies.</p>", "proof": "<p>Lemma 2.1 proves the geometric assertions and that at a spatial point at most one initial prototype is nonzero modulo periodicity. Lemma 2.2 proves all integrability, support, truncation, and seed assertions.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 2.1", "orig": "Lemma 4.1, Disjoint enlargements", "depth": 1, "snippet": "With Proposition 2's definitions, for \\(0\\le t\\le\\bar T\\), the sets \\(\\mathcal O_i(t)\\) are pairwise disjoint, and \\( \\widehat E_j^\\sigma \\subset\\{s_j<|y|<e^\\eta s_j\\}\\subset B(0,s_0). \\) Thus at each point of the lift …", "statement": "<p>With Proposition 2's definitions, for \\(0\\le t\\le\\bar T\\), the sets \\(\\mathcal O_i(t)\\) are pairwise disjoint, and\n\\[\n\\widehat E_j^\\sigma\n\\subset\\{s_j&lt;|y|&lt;e^\\eta s_j\\}\\subset B(0,s_0).\n\\]\nThus at each point of the lift at most one label has an enlargement containing that point.</p>", "proof": "<p>Opposite stripes at the same scale are at distance at least \\(p_j/4\\), whereas\n\\[\n2\\delta_j=c_\\eta p_j/2&lt;p_j/4.\n\\]\nThe radial gap between adjacent cores is\n\\[\nc_\\eta(p_j+p_{j+1})&gt;\\delta_j+\\delta_{j+1}.\n\\]\nThe gaps between nonadjacent cores are larger. Since \\(\\delta_j&lt;c_\\eta p_j\\), the enlarged set remains in its original open annulus. The untranslated transverse enlargements are disjoint and lie in \\(B(0,s_0)\\), so distinct integer translates are disjoint. Finally,\n\\[\na+2\\bar T+b_{\\rm sp}=\\frac14,\n\\]\nso axial intervals for distinct \\(n_3\\) are disjoint. These facts prove the product disjointness.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 2.2", "orig": "Lemma 4.2, Integrability of the datum", "depth": 1, "snippet": "The datum and truncations in Proposition 2 satisfy its integrability and convergence assertions, including the vanishing-seed perturbations.", "statement": "<p>The datum and truncations in Proposition 2 satisfy its integrability and convergence assertions, including the vanishing-seed perturbations.</p>", "proof": "<p>Integration over one period and summation over integer copies equal integration of each unshifted prototype on \\(\\mathbb R_x^3\\). Its transverse area is at most \\(C_\\eta s^2\\), and\n\\[\n\\int_{-a}^aS(\\gamma z/s)\\,dz\n\\le\\frac s\\gamma\\int_\\mathbb RS(\\ell)\\,d\\ell=\\frac{2s}{\\gamma}.\n\\]\nThe velocity factor has mass one, so each annular sign has mass at most \\(Cs^2\\). Its velocities obey\n\\[\n|v|\\le1+\\alpha+d_s&lt;2,\n\\]\ngiving the same energy bound.</p><p>On the positive-density set, split its logarithm into\n\\[\n\\log((K/s)d_s^{-3}),\\qquad \\log S(\\gamma z/s),\\qquad \\log J(w).\n\\]\nThe first is bounded in absolute value by \\(C(1+|\\log s|)\\);\n\\[\n\\int_\\mathbb RS|\\log S|&lt;\\infty\n\\]\nbecause its tails are bounded by \\(C(1+|\\ell|)e^{-2|\\ell|}\\); and\n\\[\n\\int J|\\log J|&lt;\\infty\n\\]\nbecause \\(J\\) is bounded and compactly supported. The annular absolute entropy is therefore at most\n\\[\nCs^2(1+|\\log s|).\n\\]\nSummation over the geometric scales converges. Disjoint supports give convergence of truncated mass, energy, and absolute entropy. Since \\(-\\log M\\) is a constant plus \\(|v|^2/2\\), relative entropy converges as well.</p><p>For \\(h_N^0=\\lambda_Ns_N\\chi(x/s_N,v)\\),\n\\[\n\\int h_N^0(1+|v|^2)\\le Cs_N^4,\\qquad\n\\int h_N^0|\\log h_N^0|\n\\le Cs_N^4(1+|\\log s_N|),\n\\]\nuniformly in \\(\\lambda_N\\in[0,L_0]\\). Moreover,\n\\[\n0\\le F_0^{(N)}\\le Cs_N^{-31}.\n\\]\nFor \\(f,b\\ge0\\), integration of \\(1+\\log(f+r)\\) on \\(0&lt;r&lt;b\\) gives\n\\[\n|(f+b)\\log(f+b)-f\\log f|\n\\le b\\{2+\\log^+(f+b)\\}+b|\\log b|.\n\\]\nThe negative logarithm is controlled by\n\\[\n\\int_0^b\\log^-(f+r)\\,dr\n\\le\\int_0^b\\log^-r\\,dr\\le b(1+|\\log b|).\n\\]\nOn the seed support,\n\\[\n\\log^+(F_0^{(N)}+h_N^0)\\le C(1+|\\log s_N|).\n\\]\nThus the entropy perturbation is \\(O(s_N^4(1+|\\log s_N|))\\); the remaining Maxwellian-reference terms follow from mass and energy.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 3", "orig": "Proposition 4.4, Uniform cold control; the additional survival conclusion is original Corollary 4.5", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p>Use Propositions 1–2. Retain\n\\[\nI_N=\\{(j,\\sigma,n):1\\le j\\le N,\\ \\sigma=\\pm1,\\ n\\in\\mathbb Z^3\\}.\n\\]\nFor nonnegative cold colors \\(c_i\\), put \\(c=\\sum_i c_i\\), let \\(h\\ge0\\), and set \\(F=c+h\\). The colored equations are original equation (2.4):\n\\[\nD_tc_i=P^b(c_i,c_i)-c_i\\nu_F^b,\n\\]\n\\[\nD_th=2P^b(c,h)+P^b(h,h)+\\sum_{i\\ne j}P^b(c_i,c_j)-h\\nu_F^b.\n\\]\nThe cross-color sum is ordered. Their sum is \\(D_tF=Q^b(F,F)\\). Integer-copy labels are covariant in the lift, and their sums are periodic. Consider such mild solutions with initial colors \\(c_i^0\\), a common finite cutoff \\(b&gt;0\\), and bounded total weighted norm on their existence interval.</p><p>Define\n\\[\nf_i(t,x,v)=c_i^0(x-tv,v).\n\\]\nFor a scale \\(s\\) and sign \\(\\sigma\\), let\n\\[\nu_s^\\sigma(t,z)=a_s^\\sigma(z-tu_s^\\sigma(t,z)).\n\\]\nFor \\(i=(j,\\sigma,n)\\), write\n\\[\nu_i(t,z)=u_{s_j}^\\sigma(t,z-n_3),\\qquad\n\\tau_i=z-n_3-tu_i,\\qquad w_i=v-u_ie_3,\n\\]\n\\[\nD_{y,i}=\\operatorname{dist}(y-\\bar n,E_j^\\sigma),\\qquad\nD_{z,i}=\\operatorname{dist}(\\tau_i,[-a,a]).\n\\]\nFor \\(c_{\\rm sp}=1/8\\) and a fixed sufficiently large \\(C_m\\), set\n\\[\nm_i=C_m\\frac K{s_j}d_{s_j}^{-3}e^t\n\\exp\\left[-\\frac{(2-t)|w_i|^2}{d_{s_j}^2}\n-\\frac{c_{\\rm sp}(D_{y,i}^2+D_{z,i}^2)}{d_{s_j}^2t}\\right],\n\\]\nwith trace at zero defined along free characteristics.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There is a fixed \\(L\\), independent of \\(N,b,h\\), such that, after imposing\n\\[\nLd_0\\bar s^9\\le1,\n\\]\nthe following hold throughout existence in \\([0,\\bar T]\\):\n\\[\n0\\le c_i\\le f_i+\\varepsilon_jm_i,\\qquad\n\\varepsilon_j=Ld_{s_j}/s_j.\n\\]\nAt a point \\((t,x)\\), call the unique retained label with \\(x\\in\\mathcal O_i(t)\\), if present, the main label. Let \\(c_{\\rm main}\\) be that color, or zero, and \\(e=c-c_{\\rm main}\\). For every \\(A\\ge0\\),\n\\[\n0\\le e\\le C_At^Ae^{-4|v|^2},\n\\]\nalso for any subset of escaped labels. If the main scale is \\(s\\), its velocity measure is bounded by \\(C/(t+s)\\) times a fixed sum of admissible sheets. Its remainder has the smaller coefficient \\(Cd_s/s^2\\), and for \\(k\\ge0\\),\n\\[\n\\int\\langle v\\rangle^kc_{\\rm main}\\,dv\\le\\frac{C_k}{t+s},\n\\]\n\\[\n\\int_{|v|&gt;R}\\langle v\\rangle^kc_{\\rm main}\\,dv\n\\le\\frac{C_ke^{-c_kR^2}}{t+s}\\qquad(R\\ge3).\n\\]\nFor every \\(A\\ge0\\),\n\\[\n\\sum_{i\\ne j}P^b(c_i,c_j)\\le C_At^{A-1}H\\qquad(t&gt;0).\n\\]</p><p>If, additionally, on \\([0,T]\\subset[0,\\bar T]\\),\n\\[\n0\\le h\\le(t+s_N)H,\n\\]\nthen for every \\(M\\ge0\\),\n\\[\ne^{-E_{s,N}(t)}f_i\\le c_i\\le f_i+L(d_s/s)m_i,\n\\]\n\\[\nE_{s,N}(t)=C(d_st/s+t^2+s_Nt)+C_Mt^{M+1}.\n\\]\nIf \\(r\\to0\\), \\(s_N\\le r\\), \\(s/r\\in[\\epsilon,R]\\), and \\(t\\le\\min(T,Ar)\\), then uniformly on these scales and free cores,\n\\[\nE_{s,N}(t)\\to0,\\qquad \\frac{td_s}{s^2}\\le C_{\\epsilon,R,A}r^9\\to0,\n\\qquad r\\int|c_i-f_i|\\,dv\\to0.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Lemmas 3.1–3.5 prove every part of original Proposition 4.4, with constants fixed before the final reduction of \\(d_0\\). Lemma 3.6 adds precisely original Corollary 4.5 under the cap assumption.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 3.1", "orig": "Lemma 4.3, Barrier identities", "depth": 1, "snippet": "For the profiles and barriers of Proposition 3, \\(u_s^\\sigma\\) is uniquely defined for all \\(t\\ge0\\), \\(|u_s^\\sigma|\\le1+\\alpha\\), and \\(C_m\\) can be chosen so that \\(f_i\\le m_i\\).", "statement": "<p>For the profiles and barriers of Proposition 3, \\(u_s^\\sigma\\) is uniquely defined for all \\(t\\ge0\\), \\(|u_s^\\sigma|\\le1+\\alpha\\), and \\(C_m\\) can be chosen so that \\(f_i\\le m_i\\). Almost everywhere along free characteristics,\n\\[\nD_tm_i\\ge\\left(1+\\frac78\\frac{|w_i|^2}{d_{s_j}^2}\\right)m_i.\n\\]\nFor a label-independent \\(C_\\nu\\),\n\\[\nP(m_i,m_i)=m_i\\nu_{m_i},\\qquad\n\\nu_{m_i}(v)\\le C_\\nu\\frac K{s_j}(d_{s_j}+|w_i|).\n\\]\nEvery free core remains in its enlargement:\n\\[\nf_i(t,x,v)&gt;0\\implies x\\in\\mathcal O_i(t).\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>The map \\(\\tau\\mapsto\\tau+ta_s^\\sigma(\\tau)\\) is strictly increasing, has derivative at least one, and maps \\(\\mathbb R\\) onto itself. Suppress indices, put \\(d=d_s\\), and let \\(A=(a_s^\\sigma)'(\\tau)\\ge0\\). Implicit differentiation gives\n\\[\nu_z=\\frac A{1+tA}\\ge0,\\quad u_t=-uu_z,\\quad\nD_tu=(v_3-u)u_z,\\quad\nD_t\\tau=\\frac{v_3-u}{1+tA}.\n\\]\nConsequently,\n\\[\nD_t|w|^2=-2u_zw_3^2,\\quad\n|D_tD_y|\\le|\\bar w|,\\quad\n|D_tD_z|\\le|w_3|/(1+tA)\\le|w_3|.\n\\]\nDifferentiating the barrier logarithm and using \\(r^2-2rq\\ge-q^2\\) for the two spatial terms yields\n\\[\nD_t\\log m\\ge\n1+(1-c_{\\rm sp})|w|^2/d^2+2(2-t)u_zw_3^2/d^2.\n\\]\nThe last term is nonnegative on the preliminary interval.</p><p>For a core flight from \\((y^0,z^0)\\), write\n\\[\nv=a_s^\\sigma(z^0-n_3)e_3+dw^0,\\qquad |w^0|\\le1.\n\\]\nThe map \\(p\\mapsto p-a_s^\\sigma(z-n_3-tp)\\) has derivative at least one, so\n\\[\n|v_3-u|\\le d|w_3^0|,\\quad |\\bar v|=d|\\bar w^0|,\n\\quad D_y\\le td|\\bar w^0|,\\quad D_z\\le td|w_3^0|.\n\\]\nThe barrier exponential is bounded below by \\(e^{-3}\\). Taking \\(C_m\\ge e^3\\|J\\|_\\infty\\) proves domination and its initial trace, without differentiating a spatial indicator.</p><p>At fixed \\((t,x)\\), \\(m_i\\) is a scalar multiple of a Maxwellian centered at \\(u_ie_3\\). Elastic conservation gives \\(m_i'm_{i,*}'=m_im_{i,*}\\). Its mass is at most \\(CK/s_j\\), and its first moment about the center is at most \\(CKd_{s_j}/s_j\\), proving the frequency estimate.</p><p>Finally,\n\\[\nD_y\\le\\bar T d_s\\le\\delta_j/2,\n\\]\nand the free-core axial speed is less than two. Hence the flight lies in \\(\\mathcal O_i(t)\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 3.2", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Cold comparison, the first part of original Proposition 4.4.", "statement": "<p>Cold comparison, the first part of original Proposition 4.4.</p><p>Under Proposition 3's assumptions, a fixed \\(L\\) can be chosen before decreasing \\(d_0\\) so that\n\\[\nc_i\\le f_i+L(d_{s_j}/s_j)m_i\n\\]\nfor every retained label throughout its bounded-weighted-norm existence interval.</p>", "proof": "<p>Set \\(U_i=f_i+\\varepsilon_jm_i\\). Since \\(\\varepsilon_j\\le1\\), Lemma 3.1 gives\n\\[\nP^b(U_i,U_i)\\le4C_\\nu\\frac K{s_j}(d_{s_j}+|w_i|)m_i,\n\\]\nwhile\n\\[\nD_tU_i\\ge L\\frac{d_{s_j}}{s_j}\n\\left(1+\\frac78|w_i|^2/d_{s_j}^2\\right)m_i.\n\\]\nChoose \\(L\\) at least \\(4C_\\nu K\\) times the supremum of\n\\[\n\\frac{1+r}{1+\\frac78r^2},\\qquad r\\ge0.\n\\]\nThen \\(D_tU_i\\ge P^b(U_i,U_i)\\).</p><p>Because \\(D_tc_i\\le P^b(c_i,c_i)\\), integration along free paths and positivity give, for \\(z_i=(c_i-U_i)_+\\),\n\\[\nz_i(t)\\le\\int_0^t\n\\bigl[2P^b(U_i,z_i)+P^b(z_i,z_i)\\bigr]\n(\\tau,x-(t-\\tau)v,v)\\,d\\tau.\n\\]\nFor each fixed scale, \\(m_i/H\\) is bounded. Proposition 1's quadratic estimate and boundedness of \\(c_i+U_i\\) give\n\\[\n\\|z_i(t)/H\\|_\\infty\n\\le C_I\\int_0^t\\|z_i(\\tau)/H\\|_\\infty\\,d\\tau.\n\\]\nIteration or an integrating factor gives \\(z_i=0\\). No hot bound is used.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 3.3", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Escaped-color estimates, the second part of original Proposition 4.4.", "statement": "<p>Escaped-color estimates, the second part of original Proposition 4.4.</p><p>Under Proposition 3's assumptions,\n\\[\ne(t,x,v)\\le C_At^Ae^{-4|v|^2}\n\\]\nfor every \\(A\\ge0\\), uniformly in \\(N,b\\). The estimate remains valid for any subset of escaped labels. Restricting further to scales \\(s_j\\le\\rho\\) gives, for every \\(B&gt;0\\), an additional factor bounded by \\(C_{A,B}\\rho^B\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>For a nonmain label, either \\(D_{y,i}\\ge\\delta_j\\) or \\(D_{z,i}\\ge b_{\\rm sp}\\). Hence\n\\[\nD_{y,i}^2+D_{z,i}^2\\ge(c_\\eta/4)^2s_j^4.\n\\]\nFor \\(a_v=(2-t)/d_s^2\\ge8\\), completing the square gives\n\\[\n\\sup_v e^{4|v|^2-a_v|v-ue_3|^2}\n=\\exp\\!\\left(\\frac{4a_v|u|^2}{a_v-4}\\right)\n\\le e^{8(1+\\alpha)^2}.\n\\]\nSince \\(c_i\\le2m_i\\), its escaped contribution is bounded by\n\\[\nCs_j^{-31}e^{-4|v|^2}\n\\exp\\!\\left[-\\frac{c_{\\rm sp}(D_{y,i}^2+D_{z,i}^2)}{d_{s_j}^2t}\\right].\n\\]</p><p>The lattice sum is uniform in the scale. Indeed,\n\\[\nD_{y,i}^2\\ge\\sum_{k=1}^2(|y_k-n_k|-s_0)_+^2,\\qquad\nD_{z,i}\\ge(|z-n_3|-a-2\\bar T)_+,\n\\]\nand comparison of monotone Gaussian tails with their integrals gives\n\\[\n\\sup_{\\xi\\in\\mathbb R}\\sum_{n\\in\\mathbb Z}\ne^{-\\beta(|\\xi-n|-R)_+^2/\\varepsilon^2}\n\\le2R+5+\\varepsilon\\sqrt{\\pi/\\beta}.\n\\]\nUse half the spatial exponent for the lattice sum and half for the escaped distance:\n\\[\n\\sum_{n:\\,i\\ {\\rm nonmain}}\ne^{-c_{\\rm sp}(D_{y,i}^2+D_{z,i}^2)/(d_s^2t)}\n\\le C e^{-\\beta_0/(s^{16}t)}.\n\\]\nFor \\(p\\ge A\\) with \\(16p-31\\ge B\\), the inequality\n\\[\ne^{-x}\\le(p/e)^px^{-p}\n\\]\nimplies\n\\[\ns^{-31}e^{-\\beta_0/(s^{16}t)}\\le C_{A,B}t^As^B.\n\\]\nSummation over the geometric scales proves all assertions and absorbs any fixed additional inverse power of \\(s_j\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 3.4", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Free-core Jacobian and sheet control, the third part of original Proposition 4.4.", "statement": "<p>Free-core Jacobian and sheet control, the third part of original Proposition 4.4.</p><p>At fixed \\((t,x)\\), for a label of scale \\(s\\), write \\(z_c=z-n_3\\), \\(y_c=y-\\bar n\\), \\(d=d_s\\). Let\n\\[\np_3=\\Phi(w_3)=a_s^\\sigma(z_c-t\\Phi(w_3))+dw_3,\\qquad\n\\bar p=d\\bar w.\n\\]\nThen the exact velocity measure of the free core is\n\\[\nf_i(t,x,p)\\,dp\n=\\frac{KS(\\gamma(z_c-tp_3)/s)}\n{s+t\\alpha\\gamma S(\\gamma(z_c-tp_3)/s)}\nJ(w)\\mathbf1_{E_j^\\sigma}(y_c-td\\bar w)\n\\mathbf1_{(-a,a)}(z_c-tp_3)\\,dw.\n\\]\nIt is dominated by \\(C/(t+s)\\) times an admissible sheet. The remainder\n\\[\nL(d_s/s)m_i\\,dp\n\\]\nhas sheet amplitude \\(Cd_s/s^2\\). The moment and Gaussian-tail bounds in Proposition 3 follow, as do the normalized kernel and parameter-tail bounds with these amplitudes.</p>", "proof": "<p>The derivative of the implicit map is\n\\[\n\\Phi'(w_3)=\n\\frac d{1+t\\alpha\\gamma S(\\gamma(z_c-t\\Phi(w_3))/s)/s}.\n\\]\nChanging all three velocity variables gives the exact formula. For \\(k_0=\\alpha\\gamma&gt;0\\) and \\(0&lt;S\\le1\\),\n\\[\n\\frac{KS}{s+tk_0S}\n\\le\\frac K{s+tk_0}\n\\le\\frac{K\\max(1,k_0^{-1})}{s+t}.\n\\]\nThus the estimate is uniform even when \\(t/s\\) is large or \\(S\\) is small.</p><p>Choose \\(C_J\\) such that\n\\[\nJ(w)\\le C_J\\pi^{-1}e^{-|\\bar w|^2}\\pi^{-1/2}e^{-w_3^2}.\n\\]\nThe axial map depends only on \\(w_3\\), and\n\\[\n|\\Phi(w_3)-u_s^\\sigma(t,z_c)|\\le d|w_3|.\n\\]\nIts axial probability image satisfies\n\\[\n\\int e^{8p_3^2}\\rho(dp_3)\n\\le e^{16(1+\\alpha)^2}\n\\int e^{16d^2w_3^2}\\pi^{-1/2}e^{-w_3^2}\\,dw_3\\le C.\n\\]\nDiscarding masks gives the sheet domination. No axial density bound is needed.</p><p>For the remainder, put \\(p=u_ie_3+d_sw\\):\n\\[\nL(d_s/s)m_i\\,dp\n\\le C\\frac{d_s}{s^2}e^{-(2-t)|w|^2}\\,dw.\n\\]\nAlso,\n\\[\n(d_s/s^2)(t+s)=d_0s^8(t+s)\\le d_0\\bar s^8(\\bar T+\\bar s),\n\\]\nso this coefficient is at most \\(C/(t+s)\\). The width restrictions permit all fixed Gaussian widenings. Lemma 1.5 gives parameter-tail bounds \\(Ce^{-cR^2}\\) times the relevant amplitude, and Lemma 1.4 gives normalized kernel tails.</p><p>The core is supported in \\(|p|&lt;2\\). The remainder has a uniformly narrow Gaussian about \\(|p_3|\\le1+\\alpha\\). Substitution in its Gaussian integral proves the moment and velocity-tail estimates.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 3.5", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Mixed-cold source and spatial moments, the fourth part of original Proposition 4.4.", "statement": "<p>Mixed-cold source and spatial moments, the fourth part of original Proposition 4.4.</p><p>Under Proposition 3's assumptions,\n\\[\n\\sum_{i\\ne j}P^b(c_i,c_j)\\le C_At^{A-1}H.\n\\]\nFor every fixed \\(k\\ge0\\),\n\\[\n\\int\\langle v\\rangle^kc(t,x,v)\\,dv\n\\le C_k\\sum_{i\\in I_N}\\frac{\\mathbf1_{\\mathcal O_i(t)}(x)}{t+s_i}\n+C_{A,k}t^A.\n\\]\nAt most one term in the displayed sum is nonzero. In one periodic cell, the enlargement of an annular sign, including its copies, has volume at most \\(Cs_i^2\\). The integrated velocity tails satisfy the same bound with a factor tending to zero with the velocity cutoff.</p>", "proof": "<p>At a fixed point, positivity and the ordered cross-color sum give\n\\[\n\\sum_{i\\ne j}P^b(c_i,c_j)\n\\le2P(c_{\\rm main},e)+P(e,e).\n\\]\nSince \\(e^{-4|v|^2}\\le CH(v)\\), Lemma 3.3 bounds \\(e\\) by \\(C_At^AH\\). Lemma 3.4 and Proposition 1 give\n\\[\nP(c_{\\rm main},H)\\le C(t+s)^{-1}H,\\qquad P(H,H)\\le CH.\n\\]\nThus, using \\(t+s\\ge t\\) and \\(t\\le1\\),\n\\[\n2P(c_{\\rm main},e)+P(e,e)\n\\le C_A\\{t^A/(t+s)+t^{2A}\\}H\n\\le C_A't^{A-1}H.\n\\]\nThe moment statement follows from Lemmas 3.3–3.4 and geometric disjointness. The volume bound follows from the transverse annular area and the bounded axial enlargement.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 3.6", "orig": "Corollary 4.5, Survival of a cold core under the cap", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Add to the preceding cold estimates the cap \\( h(t,x,v)\\le(t+s_N)H(v)\\qquad(0\\le t\\le T). \\) Then the optical-depth, stripe-displacement, and velocity-total-variation conclusions in Proposition 3 hold, uniformly in …", "statement": "<p>Add to the preceding cold estimates the cap\n\\[\nh(t,x,v)\\le(t+s_N)H(v)\\qquad(0\\le t\\le T).\n\\]\nThen the optical-depth, stripe-displacement, and velocity-total-variation conclusions in Proposition 3 hold, uniformly in \\(T,N,b\\), and the seed.</p>", "proof": "<p>The loss-integrating-factor formula gives\n\\[\nc_i(t,x,v)\\ge f_i(t,x,v)\n\\exp\\left[-\\int_0^t\\nu_F^b(\\tau,x-(t-\\tau)v,v)\\,d\\tau\\right].\n\\]\nOnly a free core needs consideration. Its entire flight lies in its own enlargement, and\n\\[\n|v-u_i(\\tau,z(\\tau))e_3|\\le d_s.\n\\]\nAt such a position, the same free core has velocities within \\(d_s\\) of this center and mass at most \\(C/(\\tau+s)\\). Its frequency is at most \\(Cd_s/s\\). The remainder has mass \\(Cd_s/s^2\\) and centered first moment \\(Cd_s^2/s^2\\); since \\(d_s/s\\le1/L\\), its frequency has the same upper bound. Every other color is escaped and has frequency at most \\(C_M\\tau^M\\). At \\(|v|&lt;2\\),\n\\[\n\\nu_h(v)\\le2\\pi(\\tau+s_N)\n\\int(|v|+|p|)H(p)\\,dp\\le C(\\tau+s_N).\n\\]\nSince \\(\\nu_F^b\\le\\nu_F\\), integration gives \\(E_{s,N}\\).</p><p>For \\(s/r\\in[\\epsilon,R]\\), \\(s_N\\le r\\), and \\(t\\le Ar\\),\n\\[\nE_{s,N}(t)\\le C_{\\epsilon,R,A}(d_s+r^2)+C_{M,A}r^{M+1}\\to0,\n\\]\nand\n\\[\ntd_s/s^2=d_0ts^8\\le d_0AR^8r^9.\n\\]\nThe sandwich gives\n\\[\n|c_i-f_i|\\le(1-e^{-E_{s,N}})f_i+L(d_s/s)m_i.\n\\]\nAfter velocity integration,\n\\[\nr\\int|c_i-f_i|\\,dv\n\\le CE_{s,N}\\frac r{t+s}+C\\frac{rd_s}{s^2}\\to0,\n\\]\nbecause \\(r/(t+s)\\le1/\\epsilon\\) and \\(rd_s/s^2\\le C_Rr^9\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 4", "orig": "Proposition 5.1, Finite approximations and cap selection", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p>Use Propositions 1–3. Retain \\(j\\le N\\), choose finite cutoffs \\(b_N\\to\\infty\\), and use the colored equations of Proposition 3. Fix\n\\[\n0\\le\\chi\\in C_c^\\infty(\\mathbb R_x^3\\times\\mathbb R_v^3),\\qquad\\chi\\not\\equiv0,\n\\]\nand periodize the hot datum\n\\[\nh(0,x,v)=\\lambda s_N\\chi(x/s_N,v).\n\\]\nDecrease \\(s_0\\) so initial seed copies are disjoint. The cold datum is independent of \\(\\lambda\\), and \\(\\|\\chi(x/s_N,v)\\|_H\\) is fixed and positive.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There is a fixed \\(T_0&gt;0\\) such that:</p><p>(a) With \\(\\lambda=0\\), the finite approximations exist on \\([0,T_0]\\) and\n\\[\n0\\le h\\le t^2H.\n\\]</p><p>(b) There is a fixed finite \\(L_0\\) such that, for every \\(0&lt;T\\le T_0\\) and every \\(N\\), some \\(\\lambda\\in[0,L_0]\\) gives an approximation on \\([0,T]\\) with\n\\[\n0\\le h(t,x,v)\\le(t+s_N)H(v),\\qquad\n\\max_{0\\le t\\le T}\\frac{\\|h(t)\\|_H}{t+s_N}=1.\n\\]\nBoth families satisfy Proposition 3's cold estimates.</p>", "proof": "<p>For fixed \\(N,b_N\\), Proposition 1 bounds gain bilinearly in \\(\\|\\cdot\\|_H\\), and\n\\[\n\\|\\nu_f^{b_N}\\|_\\infty\n\\le2\\pi b_N\\|f\\|_H\\int H.\n\\]\nThus gain and loss are locally Lipschitz on bounded weighted balls. Picard iteration along free characteristics gives local existence and uniqueness; a loss integrating factor and positive gain iteration give nonnegativity. The labeled system uses the norm of the sum of component absolute values and the analogous difference norm. The Gaussian spatial tails make the copy sums finite. Uniqueness preserves periodic covariance.</p><p>Continuation holds while the total weighted norm stays bounded, and continuous dependence follows by the same contraction and difference estimates on finitely many short intervals. Norm continuity of spatial translations is unnecessary. In particular,\n\\[\n\\|h(t)-h(s,x-(t-s)v,v)\\|_H\\le C_{N,b_N}|t-s|.\n\\]\nTransport is an isometry, so \\(t\\mapsto\\|h(t)\\|_H/(t+s_N)\\) is continuous.</p><p>Write \\(z(t)=\\|h(t)\\|_H\\). Dropping hot loss and using Proposition 3 gives the upper integral comparison\n\\[\nz'(t)\\le\\left(\\frac{C_1}{t+s_N}+C_1\\right)z(t)\n+C_1z(t)^2+C_At^{A-1}.\n\\]\nChoose \\(A&gt;C_1+2\\) and \\(D&gt;0\\) with \\((A-C_1)D&gt;C_A\\). Since \\(t/(t+s_N)\\le1\\), \\(Dt^A\\) is a supersolution after shortening a fixed preliminary interval to absorb bounded linear and quadratic terms. Shorten again until \\(Dt^A\\le t^2\\). Comparison from zero proves (a), and the cold bound ensures continuation.</p><p>Choose \\(T_0&lt;1\\), so the zero-seed cap is strict:\n\\[\n\\frac{\\|h(t)\\|_H}{t+s_N}\\le\\frac{t^2}{t+s_N}\\le T_0&lt;1.\n\\]\nChoose \\(L_0\\) with \\(L_0\\|\\chi(x/s_N,v)\\|_H&gt;1\\). For \\(\\lambda=L_0\\), the cap already fails initially.</p><p>For fixed \\(N,b_N,T\\), let \\(\\mathcal O\\subset[0,L_0]\\) be the seed parameters whose solutions exist to \\(T\\) and obey a strict cap. Continuous dependence and continuation make \\(\\mathcal O\\) relatively open. Its component containing zero has a right endpoint \\(\\lambda_*\\) strictly below \\(L_0\\). Approach \\(\\lambda_*\\) from within the component. The cap and fixed cold bounds provide a common finite weighted norm on \\([0,T]\\), with constants allowed to depend on \\(N,b_N\\). Difference estimates give a limit solution at \\(\\lambda_*\\), continuing to \\(T\\), with cap at most one. A strictly smaller maximum would extend the component past \\(\\lambda_*\\), a contradiction.</p><p>As noted in original Remark 5.2, \\(C_1\\) may be large when \\(\\alpha\\gamma\\) is small, but it is fixed before choosing \\(A,D,T_0\\). Arbitrary-power source estimates are fixed on the preliminary interval before shortening, independently of \\(N\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 5", "orig": "Proposition 6.1, Fixed-horizon limits and admissibility", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p>Fix a physical horizon \\(T\\in(0,T_0]\\), without rescaling time or space. Let \\(F_N=c_N+h_N\\) be either the zero-seed approximations or any sequence of cap shots from Proposition 4, with \\(b_N\\to\\infty\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>A subsequence converges weakly in \\(L^1([0,T]\\times\\Omega)\\) to a nonnegative density \\(F\\). The subsequence can be chosen so that\n\\[\nF_N(t)\\rightharpoonup F(t)\\quad\\text{in }L^1(\\Omega)\n\\]\nfor every \\(t\\in[0,T]\\), with uniformly small polynomially weighted velocity tails. The limit has datum \\(F_0\\) of Proposition 2 and\n\\[\nQ^\\pm(F,F)\\in L^1([0,T]\\times\\Omega),\\qquad\nF\\in C([0,T];L^1(\\Omega)).\n\\]\nIt satisfies every admissibility condition of Theorem 1 on this interval, with the inequalities at every time, and admits an admissible global continuation.</p><p>For a zero-seed sequence, the common dormant upper bound, obtained by summing the cold majorants and adding \\(t^2H\\), passes to its limit.</p>", "proof": "<p>Lemmas 5.1–5.2 establish uniform estimates. Lemmas 5.3–5.4 give compactness and identify the nonlinear equation. Lemma 5.5 supplies every-time traces, strong continuity, and finite-interval admissibility. Lemma 5.6 proves global continuation and preservation of the dormant majorant.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 5.1", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Conservation and entropy before passage to the limit, original Section 6.1.", "statement": "<p>Conservation and entropy before passage to the limit, original Section 6.1.</p><p>For each fixed approximation in Proposition 5, mass, total momentum, and energy are conserved at every time, and the local mass equation holds. With \\(\\mathcal D_b\\) denoting cutoff entropy dissipation,\n\\[\n\\mathcal H_M(F_N(t))+\\int_0^t\\mathcal D_{b_N}(F_N(s))\\,ds\n\\le\\mathcal H_M(F_N(0))\n\\]\nfor every \\(t\\in[0,T]\\). Initial mass, energy, and relative entropy converge to those of \\(F_0\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>At fixed \\(N,b_N\\), the previous estimates give \\(F_N\\le A_NH\\), all polynomial velocity moments, an \\(L^p\\) majorant for some \\(p&gt;1\\), and integrable cutoff collision terms with polynomial weights. Collision symmetry gives\n\\[\n\\int\\psi Q^{b_N}(F_N,F_N)\\,dv\n=\\frac12\\int |a|\\mathbf1_{\\{|v-v_*|&lt;b_N\\}}\nF_NF_{N,*}(\\psi'+\\psi_*'-\\psi-\\psi_*)\\,dv\\,dv_*\\,d\\omega.\n\\]\nThe right side vanishes for \\(1,v_j,|v|^2\\). Integrating the path equation and using periodicity proves the conserved quantities. A spatially dependent mass test gives local mass.</p><p>For entropy at zeros, put\n\\[\nE(v)=e^{-2|v|^2},\\qquad F_{N,\\delta}=F_N+\\delta E,\\qquad0&lt;\\delta\\le1,\n\\]\n\\[\n\\mathcal A_\\delta(f,v)=M(v)+\\int_0^f\\log\\frac{u+\\delta E(v)}{M(v)}\\,du.\n\\]\nAt the fixed approximation,\n\\[\n\\left|\\log\\frac{F_{N,\\delta}}M\\right|\n\\le C_N(1+|\\log\\delta|+|v|^2+\\log^+|\\bar v|^{-1}).\n\\]\nThe transverse logarithm is integrable against \\(H\\), since\n\\[\n\\int_0^1r^{1-q}|\\log r|\\,dr&lt;\\infty.\n\\]\nAs \\(E\\le CH\\), the fixed-cutoff bilinear estimates yield\n\\[\n|Q^{b_N}(F_{N,\\delta},F_{N,\\delta})\n-Q^{b_N}(F_N,F_N)|\\le C_{N,b_N}\\delta H.\n\\]\nReplacing the collision operator in its logarithmic integral therefore costs at most \\(C_{N,b_N,T}\\delta(1+|\\log\\delta|)\\). The path chain rule and collision entropy identity give\n\\[\n\\int_\\Omega\\mathcal A_\\delta(F_N(t),v)\n+\\int_0^t\\mathcal D_{b_N}(F_{N,\\delta}(s))\\,ds\n\\le\n\\int_\\Omega\\mathcal A_\\delta(F_N(0),v)\n+C_{N,b_N,T}\\delta(1+|\\log\\delta|).\n\\]\nThe \\(\\log M\\) term cancels by mass and energy. As \\(\\delta\\downarrow0\\), the primitives converge in integral to \\(f\\log(f/M)-f+M\\); their negative parts are controlled by the second moment. Fatou on nonnegative dissipation proves the inequality. Proposition 2 gives initial convergence, including seed overlap entropy.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 5.2", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Uniform integrability of densities and collision products, original Section 6.2.", "statement": "<p>Uniform integrability of densities and collision products, original Section 6.2.</p><p>The sequences\n\\[\n\\langle v\\rangle^kF_N,\\qquad\n\\langle v\\rangle^k\\langle w\\rangle^kF_N(t,x,v)F_N(t,x,w),\n\\qquad\n\\langle v\\rangle^kQ_N^\\pm\n\\]\nare uniformly integrable in their respective full spacetime variables and have uniformly small velocity tails, for every fixed \\(k\\ge0\\), where\n\\[\nQ_N^\\pm=Q^{\\pm,b_N}(F_N,F_N).\n\\]\nWeighted one-particle uniform integrability and velocity tightness also hold uniformly at individual times. In particular, integrals of \\(Q_N^\\pm\\) over time intervals whose lengths tend to zero vanish uniformly in \\(N\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Let \\(\\mathcal A_s=\\{s&lt;|y|&lt;e^\\eta s\\}\\subset\\mathbb T^2\\). These annuli are disjoint, contain both enlarged stripe signs at scale \\(s\\), and have area at most \\(Cs^2\\). Proposition 3 and the caps give\n\\[\nR_{k,N}(t,x):=\\int\\langle v\\rangle^kF_N\\,dv\n\\le C_k\\left(1+\\sum_{j\\le N}\\frac{\\mathbf1_{\\mathcal A_{s_j}}(y)}{t+s_j}\\right).\n\\]\nFor one annulus,\n\\[\n\\int_0^T\\frac{Cs^2}{(t+s)^2}\\,dt\n=Cs^2\\left(\\frac1s-\\frac1{T+s}\\right)\\le Cs.\n\\]\nThus, for \\(\\mathcal A_{&lt;\\varepsilon}=\\bigcup_{s_j&lt;\\varepsilon}\\mathcal A_{s_j}\\),\n\\[\n\\sup_N\\int_0^T\\int_{\\mathcal A_{&lt;\\varepsilon}\\times\\mathbb T}\nR_{k,N}^2\\,dx\\,dt\\longrightarrow0.\n\\]\nOutside these annuli only finitely many main scales occur. Their fixed positive widths give an \\(N,t,x\\)-independent majorant\n\\[\nF_N(t,x,v)\\le\\Psi_\\varepsilon(v),\\qquad\n\\int\\langle v\\rangle^k\\Psi_\\varepsilon(v)\\,dv&lt;\\infty\n\\]\nfor every \\(k\\). A finite sum of broadened Gaussians and a multiple of \\(H\\) suffices.</p><p>The removed-annulus integral of the pair product is bounded by the preceding square estimate; elsewhere it is dominated by\n\\[\n\\langle v\\rangle^k\\Psi_\\varepsilon(v)\n\\langle w\\rangle^k\\Psi_\\varepsilon(w).\n\\]\nThis proves pair-product uniform integrability and tightness. First powers give spacetime one-particle uniform integrability. At individual times,\n\\[\n\\sup_{N,\\,0\\le t\\le T}\n\\int_{\\mathcal A_{&lt;\\varepsilon}\\times\\mathbb T}R_{k,N}\\,dx\n\\le C_k\\sum_{s_j&lt;C\\varepsilon}s_j+C_k|\\mathcal A_{&lt;\\varepsilon}|\\to0.\n\\]</p><p>Collision symmetry and energy conservation give\n\\[\n\\int\\langle v\\rangle^kQ_N^\\pm\\,dv\n\\le C_kR_{k+1,N}^2.\n\\]\nOutside the removed annuli,\n\\[\nQ_N^+\\le P(\\Psi_\\varepsilon,\\Psi_\\varepsilon),\\qquad\nQ_N^-\\le\\Psi_\\varepsilon\\nu_{\\Psi_\\varepsilon}.\n\\]\nThese fixed majorants are integrable with every polynomial weight by the same collision-moment estimate. This proves collision uniform integrability, tails, and uniform absolute continuity of time integrals.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 5.3", "orig": "Lemma 6.2, An elementary averaging argument", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Let \\(f_n,g_n\\) be locally uniformly integrable in \\(L^1\\) on an open time-space-velocity region, and suppose \\( (\\partial_t+v\\cdot\\nabla_x)f_n=g_n \\) there in distributions.", "statement": "<p>Let \\(f_n,g_n\\) be locally uniformly integrable in \\(L^1\\) on an open time-space-velocity region, and suppose\n\\[\n(\\partial_t+v\\cdot\\nabla_x)f_n=g_n\n\\]\nthere in distributions. For every smooth compactly supported velocity test \\(\\psi\\), the averages\n\\[\n\\int\\psi(v)f_n(t,x,v)\\,dv\n\\]\nare relatively compact in local \\(L^1_{t,x}\\), wherever the hypotheses hold for velocities in a neighborhood of \\(\\operatorname{supp}\\psi\\). This applies on the torus and locally on \\(\\mathbb R^3\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Choose smooth interior cutoffs in time, space, and velocity, omitting the spatial cutoff on the torus. Let \\(q_n\\) be the localized sequence, extended by zero, and\n\\[\nr_n=(1+\\partial_t+v\\cdot\\nabla_x)q_n.\n\\]\nIt is uniformly integrable in \\(L^1\\), with fixed bounded time and velocity support and bounded spatial support in the Euclidean case. The identity\n\\[\nq_n(t,x,v)=\\int_0^\\infty e^{-s}r_n(t-s,x-sv,v)\\,ds\n\\]\ndefines an \\(L^1\\)-contractive damped inverse \\(\\mathcal R\\).</p><p>Truncate \\(r_n\\) at height \\(L\\), preserving sign. Then\n\\[\n\\sup_n\\|r_n-r_n^{(L)}\\|_1\\to0,\\qquad\n\\sup_n\\|r_n^{(L)}\\|_2&lt;\\infty\n\\]\nfor fixed \\(L\\). The average error after \\(\\mathcal R\\) is small in \\(L^1\\).</p><p>For \\(u_n=\\mathcal Rr_n^{(L)}\\), Fourier transformation in \\((t,x)\\) and Cauchy–Schwarz give\n\\[\n\\left|\\int\\psi(v)\\widehat u_n(\\tau,k,v)\\,dv\\right|^2\n\\le I_\\psi(\\tau,k)\\int|\\widehat r_n^{(L)}(\\tau,k,v)|^2\\,dv,\n\\]\n\\[\nI_\\psi(\\tau,k)=\\int\\frac{|\\psi(v)|^2}{1+(\\tau+k\\cdot v)^2}\\,dv.\n\\]\nIf \\(\\operatorname{supp}\\psi\\subset\\{|v|\\le R_0\\}\\), integration in the \\(k\\)-direction gives \\(I_\\psi\\le C_\\psi/|k|\\) for \\(k\\ne0\\); if \\(|\\tau|&gt;2R_0|k|\\), then \\(I_\\psi\\le C_\\psi/(1+|\\tau|^2)\\). Hence high-frequency \\(L^2\\) tails vanish uniformly. Smooth convolution approximates the averages uniformly in \\(L^2\\); on compact sets the convolutions are bounded and equicontinuous. They are relatively compact, first for fixed \\(L\\), and then in \\(L^1\\) after removing the truncation.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 5.4", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Identification of nonlinear terms, original Section 6.4.", "statement": "<p>Identification of nonlinear terms, original Section 6.4.</p><p>After subselection under Proposition 5's assumptions,\n\\[\nF_N(t,x,v)F_N(t,x,w)\n\\rightharpoonup F(t,x,v)F(t,x,w)\n\\]\nin full spacetime \\(L^1\\), also with fixed polynomial weights as needed, and\n\\[\nQ_N^\\pm\\rightharpoonup Q^\\pm(F,F)\\quad\\text{in }L^1([0,T]\\times\\Omega).\n\\]\nThe limit obeys the unrenormalized distributional equation with initial datum \\(F_0\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Lemma 5.2 supplies weak \\(L^1\\) compactness of \\(F_N\\). Since \\(D_tF_N=Q_N^+-Q_N^-\\), Lemma 5.3 gives strong local \\(L^1_{t,x}\\) convergence of compact velocity averages, after diagonal extraction for a countable dense family.</p><p>Uniform integrability gives a weak limit \\(W\\) of\n\\[\nW_N=F_N(t,x,v)F_N(t,x,w).\n\\]\nFor separated compact velocity tests \\(\\psi,\\chi\\),\n\\[\n\\int\\psi(v)\\chi(w)W_N\\,dv\\,dw\n=\\left(\\int\\psi F_N\\,dv\\right)\\left(\\int\\chi F_N\\,dw\\right).\n\\]\nThe factors converge in local \\(L^1\\), so their product converges in measure. The products are uniformly integrable by Lemma 5.2, hence converge in \\(L^1\\). It follows that\n\\[\n\\int\\psi(v)\\chi(w)W\\,dv\\,dw\n=\\left(\\int\\psi F\\,dv\\right)\\left(\\int\\chi F\\,dw\\right).\n\\]\nDensity of finite sums of separated tests identifies \\(W=F\\otimes F\\). Uniform integrability removes endpoint time intervals and velocity tails.</p><p>Adjoining angular measure preserves weak convergence. The elastic map is measure preserving and involutive, so the primed pair products have the corresponding limit. On bounded two-velocity sets \\(|a|\\) is bounded and the cutoff is eventually absent. Polynomial tails remove the truncations. Weak compactness of the collision terms then identifies their limits as \\(Q^\\pm(F,F)\\). Initial densities converge in \\(L^1\\), allowing passage of the initial boundary term.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 5.5", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Every-time traces, entropy, and the chain rule, original Section 6.5.", "statement": "<p>Every-time traces, entropy, and the chain rule, original Section 6.5.</p><p>The subsequence and limit in Lemma 5.4 have the every-time convergence, strong \\(L^1\\) continuity, finite-interval admissibility, and every-time entropy inequality asserted in Proposition 5.</p>", "proof": "<p>For smooth periodic \\(\\varphi(x,v)\\) compactly supported in velocity,\n\\[\n|\\langle F_N(t)-F_N(s),\\varphi\\rangle|\n\\le\\int_s^t\\int_\\Omega\n\\bigl(F_N|v\\cdot\\nabla_x\\varphi|+(Q_N^++Q_N^-)|\\varphi|\\bigr).\n\\]\nThe first term is bounded by \\(C_\\varphi|t-s|\\); the second has a uniform modulus by Lemma 5.2. Extract uniform convergence of pairings for a countable dense test collection. At each time, fixed-time uniform integrability gives weak \\(L^1\\) compactness; all cluster points have the same smooth pairings. One subsequence therefore converges weakly at every time and defines a time representative. Weighted tightness passes mass, momentum, and energy, so energy is conserved on this initial interval.</p><p>The function\n\\[\n\\Phi(A,B)=(A-B)\\log(A/B)\n\\]\nis jointly convex and lower semicontinuous, being the sum of two convex perspectives, and satisfies\n\\[\n\\Phi(cA,cB)=c\\Phi(A,B).\n\\]\nWeak convergence of the pair products and their primed versions therefore gives lower semicontinuity of dissipation on bounded collision sets. Exhaustion gives, for every \\(t\\),\n\\[\n\\int_0^t\\mathcal D(F(s))\\,ds\n\\le\\liminf_N\\int_0^t\\mathcal D_{b_N}(F_N(s))\\,ds.\n\\]\nThe same convex lower semicontinuity applies to \\(\\mathcal H_M(F_N(t))\\). Together with Lemma 5.1 and initial entropy convergence,\n\\[\n\\mathcal H_M(F(t))+\\int_0^t\\mathcal D(F(s))\\,ds\n\\le\\mathcal H_M(F_0).\n\\]</p><p>In free coordinates,\n\\[\n\\partial_t[F(t,x+tv,v)]\n=Q(F,F)(t,x+tv,v).\n\\]\nSubtracting the Bochner integral leaves a distribution with zero time derivative and prescribed initial value. Hence\n\\[\nF(t,x+tv,v)=F_0(x,v)\n+\\int_0^tQ(F,F)(s,x+sv,v)\\,ds\n\\]\nin \\(L^1\\), first almost everywhere in time and then for every time by the representative. The right side is absolutely continuous in \\(L^1\\), and free transport is strongly continuous on \\(L^1\\). Thus \\(F\\in C([0,T];L^1)\\).</p><p>On almost every free path, the ordinary absolutely continuous chain rule yields\n\\[\nD_t[M\\beta(F/M)]=\\beta'(F/M)Q(F,F).\n\\]\nThe source is integrable, and\n\\[\n|M\\beta(f/M)-M\\beta(g/M)|\\le C_\\beta|f-g|,\n\\qquad |M\\beta(f/M)|\\le C_\\beta f.\n\\]\nThis gives the required renormalized equation and traces. Separate renormalized collision integrability follows from \\(Q^\\pm/(1+F/M)\\le Q^\\pm\\).</p><p>Absolute collision integrability permits velocity integration, giving local mass and its initial density. Finally, split \\(\\{F&lt;1\\}\\) at \\(e^{-1-|v|^2}\\): above the threshold,\n\\[\n-F\\log F\\le(1+|v|^2)F,\n\\]\nand below it use a multiple of \\(e^{-(1+|v|^2)/2}\\). Mass, energy, and the entropy inequality therefore bound absolute entropy uniformly.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 5.6", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Continuation and inherited bounds, original Section 6.6.", "statement": "<p>Continuation and inherited bounds, original Section 6.6.</p><p>Every initial-interval solution of Lemma 5.5 has an admissible global continuation with the same terminal trace. The dormant common majorant passes to a zero-seed limit.</p>", "proof": "<p>At time \\(T\\), the density has finite mass, energy, and absolute entropy. Apply the global DiPerna–Lions theorem, in the periodic formulation cited in the paper as Levermore–Masmoudi, Theorem 4.1.</p><p>For\n\\[\nb_{\\rm phys}(\\omega,v-w)=|(v-w)\\cdot\\omega|,\n\\]\nlocal integrability holds, and for compact \\(K\\subset\\mathbb R^3\\),\n\\[\n\\frac1{1+|v|^2}\\int_{K\\times S^2}b_{\\rm phys}\\,d\\omega\\,dw\n=\\frac{2\\pi}{1+|v|^2}\\int_K|v-w|\\,dw\\to0.\n\\]\nTo match normalization, set\n\\[\nC_b=\\int b_{\\rm phys}(\\omega,v-w)M(v)M(w)\\,d\\omega\\,dv\\,dw,\n\\qquad\\epsilon=C_b^{-1}.\n\\]\nUse the normalized source kernel \\(b_{\\rm phys}/C_b\\) in\n\\[\n\\epsilon\\partial_sG+v\\cdot\\nabla_xG\n=\\epsilon^{-1}Q_{b_{\\rm phys}/C_b}(G,G),\n\\qquad G(0)=F(T)/M.\n\\]\nThen\n\\[\n\\widetilde F(t,x,v)=M(v)G(\\epsilon t,x,v)\n\\]\nhas the required collision normalization; the same substitution converts the dissipation coefficient to one.</p><p>The cited theorem provides weak \\(L^1\\) continuity, the initial trace, renormalizations with \\(|\\beta'|\\le C/\\sqrt{1+z}\\), including those required here, and local mass. Integrating its momentum equation over the torus removes the spatial divergence of the momentum defect and gives total momentum. Weak continuity and the second-moment bound extend this to every time. Dropping nonnegative defect contributions gives the required energy and entropy inequalities at every time. The preceding negative-entropy estimate gives absolute entropy bounds.</p><p>For separate global renormalized collision integrability, write \\(g=f/M\\). Since \\(f/(1+g)\\le M\\),\n\\[\n\\int_\\Omega\\frac{Q^-(f,f)}{1+g}\n\\le\\int_\\Omega M\\nu_f\n\\le C\\int_\\Omega(1+|v|)f.\n\\]\nFor \\(A,B\\ge0\\),\n\\[\nA\\le2B+\\frac2{\\log2}(A-B)\\log(A/B).\n\\]\nApply this with \\(A=f'f_*'\\), \\(B=ff_*\\), divide by \\(1+g(v)\\), and integrate:\n\\[\n\\int_\\Omega\\frac{Q^+(f,f)}{1+g}\n\\le2\\int_\\Omega\\frac{Q^-(f,f)}{1+g}\n+\\frac8{\\log2}\\mathcal D(f).\n\\]\nThe moment and entropy-dissipation bounds give integrability on every finite interval.</p><p>Concatenate \\(F\\) and \\(\\widetilde F\\) at \\(T\\). Their weak traces agree. Adding their renormalized identities cancels the common \\(M\\beta(F(T)/M)\\) term; local mass concatenates similarly. Add the continuation entropy inequality to the initial-interval inequality at \\(T\\). Energy and momentum concatenate using terminal moments.</p><p>For a dormant sequence, summing Proposition 3's cold majorants and adding \\(t^2H\\) gives a common locally integrable pointwise upper bound. Nonnegative testing and weak convergence preserve it.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 6", "orig": "Proposition 7.1, The cap dilation and its linear limit", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p>Unlike Proposition 5, let physical horizons shrink. Choose cap shots with\n\\[\nT_N\\downarrow0,\\qquad s_N/T_N\\to0,\\qquad b_N\\to\\infty,\n\\]\nand a cap time \\(t_{*,N}\\in[0,T_N]\\). In the lift centered at the origin, set\n\\[\nr_N=t_{*,N}+s_N,\\qquad\nh_{r_N}(t,x,v)=r_N^{-1}h_N(r_Nt,r_Nx,v),\n\\]\n\\[\nC_{r_N}(t,x,v)=r_Nc_N(r_Nt,r_Nx,v),\n\\]\n\\[\nl_N=s_N/r_N\\to l\\in[0,1],\\qquad\nU_N=T_N/r_N\\to U\\in[1,\\infty].\n\\]\nHere \\(r_N\\to0\\); \\(U\\ge1\\) follows from\n\\[\nU_N\\ge(1+s_N/T_N)^{-1},\n\\]\nnot from a prelimit assertion \\(U_N\\ge1\\). The dilated cap time is \\(1-l_N\\), and\n\\[\n0\\le h_{r_N}\\le(t+l_N)H\\qquad(0\\le t\\le U_N).\n\\]</p><p>After subselection of logarithmic phase, the scales \\(\\mu_{N,j}=s_j/r_N\\) converge on compact subsets of \\((0,\\infty)\\) to a geometric mesh \\(\\mathcal M\\). If \\(l&gt;0\\), it is \\(\\{l,e^\\eta l,e^{2\\eta}l,\\ldots\\}\\); if \\(l=0\\), it is two-sided with no smallest member.</p><p>For \\(\\mu&gt;0\\), \\(\\sigma=\\pm1\\), let\n\\[\nu_{\\mu,\\sigma}(t,z)\n=\\sigma+\\alpha\\tanh\\left(\\frac{\\gamma(z-tu_{\\mu,\\sigma}(t,z))}{\\mu}\\right).\n\\]\nOn \\(\\mu&lt;|y|&lt;e^\\eta\\mu\\), define\n\\[\nB(t,x,dp)=\\sum_{\\sigma=\\pm1}\nb_{\\mu,\\sigma}(t,z)\\delta_{u_{\\mu,\\sigma}(t,z)e_3}(dp),\n\\]\n\\[\nb_{\\mu,\\sigma}(t,z)=\n\\frac{\\theta KS(\\gamma(z-tu_{\\mu,\\sigma})/\\mu)}\n{\\mu+t\\alpha\\gamma S(\\gamma(z-tu_{\\mu,\\sigma})/\\mu)}.\n\\]\nSet \\(B=0\\) in \\(|y|&lt;l\\) when \\(l&gt;0\\), and choose arbitrary bounded admissible values on interfaces and the transverse axis. In particular,\n\\[\n\\int\\langle p\\rangle^kB(t,x,dp)\\le C_k/(t+\\mu)\\le C_k/t,\n\\]\nand for \\(l&gt;0\\) the bound is \\(C_k/(t+l)\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>A subsequence satisfies\n\\[\nh_{r_N}\\rightharpoonup g\n\\quad\\text{in local spacetime }L^1((0,U)\\times\\mathbb R^3\\times\\mathbb R^3),\n\\]\nwhere\n\\[\n0\\le g\\le(t+l)H,\\qquad\nD_tg=2P(B,g)-g\\nu_B.\n\\]\nThe separate collision terms are locally integrable with an integrable velocity majorant uniform in position on compact positive-time intervals. Compactly supported hot velocity averages converge strongly in local \\(L^1_{t,x}\\).</p><p>The limit has a locally strongly continuous \\(L^1\\) representative at positive times and satisfies the integrating-factor formula in Lemma 6.3. If \\(l&gt;0\\), after \\(\\lambda_N\\to\\lambda_0\\), these conclusions extend to zero with\n\\[\ng(0,x,v)=\\lambda_0l\\chi(x/l,v).\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>The mesh description follows by choosing a grid point in \\((e^{-\\eta},1]\\), passing to its limit, and multiplying by \\(e^{j\\eta}\\); when the smallest point stays positive its integer distance from this point is bounded. Coincident phase endpoints define the same mesh. The implicit profile has positive \\(u\\)-derivative\n\\[\n1+t\\alpha\\gamma S(\\gamma(z-tu)/\\mu)/\\mu,\n\\]\nso it is unique and smooth. Lemma 6.1 identifies the cold background; Lemma 6.2 proves hot compactness and the limiting equation; Lemma 6.3 proves the traces and mild formula.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 6.1", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Cold convergence, original Section 7.2.", "statement": "<p>Cold convergence, original Section 7.2.</p><p>Under Proposition 6's assumptions, for every continuous compactly supported velocity test \\(\\psi\\),\n\\[\n\\int C_{r_N}(t,x,p)\\psi(p)\\,dp\n\\rightharpoonup^*\n\\int\\psi(p)B(t,x,dp)\n\\]\nin local \\(L^\\infty_{t,x}\\) on \\((0,U)\\times\\mathbb R^3\\), with removable polynomial velocity tails. If \\(l&gt;0\\), the assertion holds down to time zero.</p>", "proof": "<p>Fix \\(a\\le t\\le A&lt;U\\), \\(|x|\\le R\\), with \\(a&gt;0\\). Eventually the cap holds on the whole physical interval \\([0,r_NA]\\). Proposition 3 gives\n\\[\n\\int\\langle p\\rangle^kC_{r_N}\\,dp\n\\le C_k\\left(\\frac1{t+l_N}+1\\right),\n\\]\nand escaped tails bounded by\n\\[\nC_Mr_N^{M+1}t^Me^{-4|p|^2}.\n\\]</p><p>First retain scales \\(\\mu_{N,j}\\in[\\varepsilon,R_1]\\); their number is uniformly finite. Proposition 3's survival bound gives\n\\[\ne^{-\\epsilon_N}c_{i,\\rm free}\\le c_i\n\\le c_{i,\\rm free}+C(d_s/s)m_i,\\qquad\\epsilon_N\\to0,\n\\]\nbecause\n\\[\nr_Nd_s/s=d_0r_N^{10}\\mu_{N,j}^9,\\qquad\nr_N^2+r_Ns_N\\le2r_N^2.\n\\]\nAfter multiplication by \\(r_N\\), the remainder has velocity mass\n\\[\nO(d_0r_N^9\\mu_{N,j}^8).\n\\]\nActual and free-core measures therefore differ by \\(o(1)\\) in velocity total variation, also with fixed polynomial weights.</p><p>For \\(\\mu_N=s/r_N\\), the exact core parameterization is\n\\[\n\\bar p=d_s\\bar w,\\qquad\np_3=\\sigma+\\alpha\\tanh(\\gamma(z-tp_3)/\\mu_N)+d_sw_3,\n\\]\nand\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int r_Nc_{i,\\rm free}(r_Nt,r_Nx,p)\\psi(p)\\,dp\n={}&amp;\\int J(w)\n\\frac{KS(\\gamma(z-tp_3)/\\mu_N)}\n{\\mu_N+t\\alpha\\gamma S(\\gamma(z-tp_3)/\\mu_N)}\n\\psi(d_s\\bar w,p_3)\\\\\n&amp;\\times\\mathbf1_E(r_Ny-r_Ntd_s\\bar w)\n\\mathbf1_{\\{|r_N(z-tp_3)|&lt;1/8\\}}\\,dw.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nThe last indicator is eventually one on the compact region. Also\n\\[\n|p_3-u_{\\mu_N,\\sigma}(t,z)|\\le d_s|w_3|.\n\\]\nThus, when \\(\\mu_N\\to\\mu&gt;0\\), the smooth amplitude and velocity test converge uniformly on \\(\\operatorname{supp}J\\).</p><p>The dilated stripe period is\n\\[\ns^2/r_N=r_N\\mu_N^2\\to0,\n\\]\nand the relative transverse displacement is bounded by\n\\[\n\\frac{Ad_s}{r_N\\mu_N^2}=Ad_0r_N^9\\mu_N^8\\to0.\n\\]\nThe translation changes stripe indicators by \\(o(1)\\) in integral on bounded sets, uniformly in phase. Annular margins have dilated width \\(O(r_N\\mu_N^2)\\), so vanish.</p><p>For the one-period stripe indicator \\(q\\), \\(q-\\theta\\) has a bounded periodic primitive \\(Q\\); hence\n\\[\n\\left|\\int f(y_1)(q(y_1/p+\\zeta)-\\theta)\\,dy_1\\right|\n\\le p\\|Q\\|_\\infty\\|f'\\|_1.\n\\]\nFubini and \\(L^1\\) approximation give weak-* convergence to \\(\\theta\\) on annular restrictions. Integrating \\(J\\) produces precisely \\(b_{\\mu,\\sigma}\\).</p><p>The region \\(|y|&lt;C\\varepsilon\\) has integral at most \\(C_{a,A,R,k}\\varepsilon^2\\) by the uniform density bound, both for the approximations and for \\(B\\). Larger scales cannot be main in the compact spatial set. This removes the scale restrictions. The common \\(L^\\infty\\) bound extends convergence from smooth tests to all \\(L^1\\) tests. If \\(l&gt;0\\), take \\(\\varepsilon&lt;l/2\\), and the same estimates hold at zero.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 6.2", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Hot compactness and cold–hot products, original Sections 7.3–7.4.", "statement": "<p>Hot compactness and cold–hot products, original Sections 7.3–7.4.</p><p>Under Proposition 6's assumptions, the hot sequence has the stated weak compactness and strong velocity-average compactness, its rescaled nonlinear errors vanish, and\n\\[\nC_{r_N}(t,x,p)h_{r_N}(t,x,v)\\,dp\\,dv\\,dx\\,dt\n\\rightharpoonup B(t,x,dp)g(t,x,v)\\,dv\\,dx\\,dt\n\\]\nagainst continuous compact velocity tests with smooth compact time-space dependence. This identifies the limiting linear equation.</p>", "proof": "<p>The exact rescaled hot equation is\n\\[\nD_th_{r_N}=2P^{b_N}(C_{r_N},h_{r_N})\n-h_{r_N}\\nu_{C_{r_N}}^{b_N}+E_N,\n\\]\n\\[\nE_N=r_N^2\\bigl[P^{b_N}(h_{r_N},h_{r_N})\n-h_{r_N}\\nu_{h_{r_N}}^{b_N}\\bigr]+S_N,\n\\]\n\\[\nS_N(t,x,v)=\\sum_{i\\ne j}P^{b_N}(c_{i,N},c_{j,N})(r_Nt,r_Nx,v).\n\\]\nThere is no additional scaling factor in \\(S_N\\). Proposition 3 gives, for large fixed \\(M\\),\n\\[\n0\\le S_N\\le C_Mr_N^{M-1}t^{M-1}H.\n\\]\nProposition 1 and the cap give\n\\[\nr_N^2P^{b_N}(h_{r_N},h_{r_N})\n\\le Cr_N^2(t+l_N)^2H,\n\\]\n\\[\nr_N^2h_{r_N}\\nu_{h_{r_N}}^{b_N}\n\\le Cr_N^2(t+l_N)^2\\langle v\\rangle H.\n\\]\nThus \\(E_N\\to0\\) locally in \\(L^1\\).</p><p>The sheet estimates give\n\\[\nP^{b_N}(C_{r_N},H)\n\\le C\\left(\\frac1{t+l_N}+1\\right)H,\n\\]\n\\[\n\\nu_{C_{r_N}}^{b_N}(v)\n\\le C\\left(\\frac1{t+l_N}+1\\right)\\langle v\\rangle.\n\\]\nConsequently, on \\(a\\le t\\le A&lt;U\\),\n\\[\n|D_th_{r_N}|\\le C_{a,A}\\langle v\\rangle H.\n\\]\nFor \\(l&gt;0\\), this holds down to zero. The cap gives weak local \\(L^1\\) compactness, and Lemma 5.3 gives strong local compactness of compact velocity averages.</p><p>For compact velocity tests \\(\\psi,\\chi\\), let\n\\[\na_N=\\int\\psi C_{r_N}\\,dp,\\qquad\n\\zeta_N=\\int\\chi h_{r_N}\\,dv.\n\\]\nLemma 6.1 gives bounded weak-* convergence \\(a_N\\rightharpoonup^*a\\), while hot averaging gives \\(\\zeta_N\\to\\zeta\\) strongly in \\(L^1\\). Therefore, for bounded \\(\\varphi(t,x)\\),\n\\[\n\\int\\varphi(a_N\\zeta_N-a\\zeta)\n=\\int\\varphi a_N(\\zeta_N-\\zeta)\n+\\int\\varphi(a_N-a)\\zeta\\to0.\n\\]\nSeparated-test approximation and uniform product moments give the claimed measure convergence.</p><p>For a continuous output test \\(\\phi\\), the weak gain test is\n\\[\n\\mathcal K_\\phi(p,v)=\\frac{|p-v|}{2}\n\\int_{S^2}\\phi\\left(\\frac{p+v}{2}+\\frac{|p-v|}{2}\\omega\\right)\\,d\\omega.\n\\]\nIt is continuous, including at \\(p=v\\), and bounded by\n\\[\n2\\pi|p-v|\\|\\phi\\|_\\infty.\n\\]\nThe loss test is \\(2\\pi|p-v|\\phi(v)\\). Moment tails remove velocity truncations and the cutoff. Each atomic beam in \\(B\\) is an allowed zero-width sheet, so\n\\[\nP(B,g)\\le C_{a,A}H,\\qquad\ng\\nu_B\\le C_{a,A}\\langle v\\rangle H.\n\\]\nThe limiting gain is therefore a density, not an additional singular measure. Passing the rescaled equation proves the linear equation.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 6.3", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Initial trace and local mild formula, original Section 7.5.", "statement": "<p>Initial trace and local mild formula, original Section 7.5.</p><p>The representative \\(g\\) in Proposition 6 is locally strongly continuous in \\(L^1\\) at positive times, with the cap at every trace. For \\(0&lt;a&lt;t&lt;U\\),\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\ng(t,x,v)={}&amp;g(a,x-(t-a)v,v)\n\\exp\\left[-\\int_a^t\\nu_B(\\tau,x-(t-\\tau)v,v)\\,d\\tau\\right]\\\\\n&amp;+2\\int_a^tP(B,g)(s,x-(t-s)v,v)\n\\exp\\left[-\\int_s^t\\nu_B(\\tau,x-(t-\\tau)v,v)\\,d\\tau\\right]\\,ds.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nIf \\(l&gt;0\\), the formula holds with \\(a=0\\), and\n\\[\ng(0,x,v)=\\lambda_0l\\chi(x/l,v).\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>For \\(l&gt;0\\), the local source majorant holds down to zero, and on compact spatial sets the rescaled initial density is eventually exactly\n\\[\nh_{r_N}(0,x,v)=\\lambda_Nl_N\\chi(x/l_N,v).\n\\]\nAfter \\(\\lambda_N\\to\\lambda_0\\), this converges strongly locally in \\(L^1\\) to the stated trace. The weak transport identity can be passed with tests touching zero.</p><p>On \\([a,A]\\subset(0,U)\\), change to free coordinates on bounded velocities and enlarged spatial compact sets. The locally integrable transport source gives a locally strongly continuous \\(L^1\\) representative. Uniform integrable velocity tails remove the velocity restriction. Along almost every free path,\n\\[\nD_tg+\\nu_Bg=2P(B,g),\n\\]\nwith \\(\\nu_B\\le C_a\\langle v\\rangle\\). The ordinary integrating factor gives the formula. Null annular interfaces do not alter it: free transport preserves phase-space measure, and Fubini discards the affected paths.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 7", "orig": "Proposition 8.1, Nonvanishing of every cap limit", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p>Consider any sequence of cap shots with horizons \\(T_n\\to0\\), indices \\(N_n\\to\\infty\\), and \\(s_{N_n}/T_n\\to0\\). Use Proposition 6's dilation and any subsequence provided there. Write\n\\[\nr_n=t_{*,n}+s_{N_n},\\quad\n\\ell_n=s_{N_n}/r_n,\\quad\n\\tau_n=t_{*,n}/r_n=1-\\ell_n,\\quad\nU_n=T_n/r_n,\n\\]\n\\[\nh_n=h_{r_n},\\qquad C_n=C_{r_n},\\qquad f_n=h_n/H.\n\\]\nThus\n\\[\n0\\le f_n\\le t+\\ell_n,\\qquad\\|f_n(\\tau_n)\\|_\\infty=1.\n\\]\nEssential norms ignore the velocity axis. Use Borel representatives obeying the asserted bounds; characteristic identities and endpoint choices are made on common full-measure sets.</p><p>For \\(0\\le t\\le1\\), let \\(i\\) be the retained label with \\(r_nx\\in\\mathcal O_i(r_nt)\\), if present, and define\n\\[\nA_n(t,x,dp)=r_nf_i(r_nt,r_nx,p)\\,dp;\n\\]\notherwise set \\(A_n=0\\). Here the \\(f_i\\) are the free cold cores of Proposition 3, not the normalized hot ratio \\(f_n\\). These measures are defined independently of the shot's existence beyond \\(U_n\\).</p><p>For a fixed lower time \\(a&gt;0\\), define\n\\[\nK_n(t,x,v;dp\\,d\\xi)\n=4A_n(t,x,dp)\\delta((v-p)\\cdot(\\xi-v))\n\\frac{H(\\xi)}{H(v)}\\,d\\xi,\n\\]\n\\[\n(\\mathcal V_nF)(t,x,v)\n=\\int_a^t\\int F(s,x-(t-s)v,\\xi)\nK_n(s,x-(t-s)v,v;dp\\,d\\xi)\\,ds.\n\\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The limit \\(g\\) of Proposition 6 satisfies\n\\[\ng\\not\\equiv0\\quad\\text{on }(0,U)\\times\\mathbb R_x^3\\times\\mathbb R_v^3.\n\\]\nThis includes \\(l=1\\), \\(U=1\\), and \\(U=\\infty\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Pass to \\(\\lambda_n\\to\\lambda_\\infty\\). If \\(l&gt;0\\) and \\(\\lambda_\\infty&gt;0\\), Proposition 6 gives the nonzero initial trace\n\\[\ng(0,x,v)=\\lambda_\\infty l\\chi(x/l,v).\n\\]\nSince\n\\[\n\\nu_B(t,x,v)\\le C(1+|v|)/(t+l),\n\\]\nits survival along bounded finite flights from zero is strictly positive, giving positive interior mass.</p><p>If \\(l&gt;0\\) and \\(\\lambda_\\infty=0\\), Lemma 7.1 from zero and \\(t+\\ell_n\\ge l/2\\) give\n\\[\n\\sup_{0\\le t\\le\\tau_n}\\|f_n(t)\\|_\\infty\n\\le(\\lambda_n\\ell_n\\|\\chi/H\\|_\\infty+\\varepsilon_n)e^{2C/l}\\to0,\n\\]\ncontradicting the cap. This includes \\(l=1\\) and hits tending to zero.</p><p>It remains that \\(l=0\\), so \\(\\tau_n\\to1\\). Suppose \\(g=0\\). Nonnegativity upgrades local weak convergence to local \\(L^1\\) convergence of \\(h_n\\); away from the axis, \\(H\\) has a positive lower bound on compact velocity sets. Hence \\(f_n\\to0\\) in measure on compact subsets of the nonaxial open region.</p><p>Fix \\(0&lt;a&lt;1/4\\). The majorizing mild inequality is\n\\[\nf_n\\le d_n+\\mathcal V_nf_n+e_n,\n\\]\nwhere\n\\[\nd_n(t,x,v)=f_n(a,x-(t-a)v,v),\\qquad\n\\|d_n\\|_\\infty\\le a+\\ell_n,\\qquad0\\le e_n\\le\\varepsilon_n.\n\\]\nIterate five times:\n\\[\nf_n(t,x,v)\\le\n(a+\\ell_n+\\varepsilon_n)\n\\sum_{j=0}^4\\frac{(C\\log(1/a))^j}{j!}\n+(\\mathcal V_n^5f_n)(t,x,v).\n\\]</p><p>Choose cap endpoints in the common full-measure set with\n\\[\nf_n(\\tau_n,x_n,v_n)\\ge1/2.\n\\]\nFor fixed \\(a\\), Lemma 7.2 excludes escaping endpoints whenever the residual has positive limsup. Extract convergent endpoints. An axial limiting velocity makes its first two gains vanish by Lemma 7.4, with later gains bounded. A nonaxial limiting velocity is covered by Lemma 7.8. Thus the residual tends to zero along the full sequence.</p><p>Taking limsup gives\n\\[\n\\frac12\\le a\\sum_{j=0}^4\\frac{(C\\log(1/a))^j}{j!}.\n\\]\nLet \\(a\\downarrow0\\), a contradiction. The target-time deletion in Lemma 7.8 handles \\(U=1\\); no step excludes \\(U=\\infty\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 7.1", "orig": "Lemma 8.2, Core kernels on the dilated scale", "depth": 1, "snippet": "For Proposition 7's \\(A_n\\), there are \\(\\varepsilon_n\\to0\\) such that, while the shot exists and \\(0<t<\\min(1,U_n)\\), \\( D_th_n\\le2P(A_n,h_n)+\\varepsilon_nH. \\) At each point there is at most one main free core, of …", "statement": "<p>For Proposition 7's \\(A_n\\), there are \\(\\varepsilon_n\\to0\\) such that, while the shot exists and \\(0&lt;t&lt;\\min(1,U_n)\\),\n\\[\nD_th_n\\le2P(A_n,h_n)+\\varepsilon_nH.\n\\]\nAt each point there is at most one main free core, of scale \\(\\mu=s/r_n\\), with \\(|y|\\asymp\\mu\\) relative to its periodic copy, and\n\\[\n\\frac{2P(A_n,H)}H\\le\\frac C{t+\\ell_n}.\n\\]\nBefore masks, that core is the pushforward of\n\\[\n\\frac{KS(\\gamma(z-tp_3)/\\mu)}\n{\\mu+t\\alpha\\gamma S(\\gamma(z-tp_3)/\\mu)}J(w)\\,dw,\n\\]\n\\[\n\\bar p=d_s\\bar w,\\qquad\np_3=\\sigma+\\alpha\\tanh(\\gamma(z-tp_3)/\\mu)+d_sw_3,\n\\]\nwith amplitude at most \\(C/(t+\\mu)\\). Masks lie in \\([0,1]\\).</p><p>For \\(t\\ge a&gt;0\\), the complete normalized kernel \\(K_n\\) has mass at most \\(C/a\\), uniformly negligible increment and Gaussian-parameter tails, decay at large outgoing velocity, and all axial and small-input conclusions of Lemma 1.4 with amplitude \\(C/a\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Proposition 3 bounds actual cold density by the main free core plus a positive remainder. The main dilated remainder has sheet amplitude\n\\[\nCr_nd_s/s^2=Cr_nd_0s^8\\le Cr_n.\n\\]\nThe escaped part is bounded by\n\\[\nC_Ar_n(r_nt)^Ae^{-4|v|^2}.\n\\]\nProposition 1 gives \\(C_n\\le A_n+R_n\\) with\n\\[\nP(R_n,H)/H=o(1)\n\\]\nuniformly down to zero. The cap gives \\(h_n\\le2H\\). The hot–hot gain is at most \\(Cr_n^2H\\), and the mixed-cold source at most \\(C_A(r_nt)^{A-1}H\\), \\(A&gt;1\\). Dropping losses proves the inequality.</p><p>The core formula is Proposition 3's exact Jacobian after multiplication by \\(r_n\\). The smallest scale is \\(\\ell_n\\), giving the gain amplitude. The coefficient four in \\(K_n\\) is the product of the hot gain coefficient two and the Carleman coefficient two. Propositions 1 and 3 give all kernel and parameter-tail conclusions, including the ratio \\(H(\\xi)/H(v)\\) under axial compression.</p><p>For finite \\(n\\), \\(A_n=a_n\\,dp\\), and its input marginal is, for \\(\\xi\\ne v\\),\n\\[\nK_n(s,x,v;\\mathbb R^3,d\\xi)\n=\\frac{4H(\\xi)}{|\\xi-v|H(v)}\n\\left[\\int_{(v-p)\\cdot(\\xi-v)=0}a_n(s,x,p)\\,dA_p\\right]d\\xi.\n\\]\nThere is no atom at \\(\\xi=v\\). Tonelli and the measure-preserving substitution \\(X=x-(t-s)v\\) show that altering inputs on null sets changes \\(\\mathcal V_nF(t)\\) only on null endpoint sets. Finite repetition and a countable choice \\(a\\downarrow0\\) provide the common full-measure endpoint sets.</p><p>Finally, ordered time integration of \\(\\prod C/t_i\\) gives\n\\[\n\\|\\mathcal V_n^jF(t)\\|_\\infty\n\\le\\|F\\|_\\infty\\frac{(C\\log(t/a))^j}{j!},\n\\qquad a\\le t\\le1.\n\\]</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 7.2", "orig": "Lemma 8.3, Endpoint and path truncations", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Fix \\(a>0\\), \\(t_n\\in[a,1]\\), and \\(0\\le F_n\\le2\\).", "statement": "<p>Fix \\(a&gt;0\\), \\(t_n\\in[a,1]\\), and \\(0\\le F_n\\le2\\). In\n\\[\n(\\mathcal V_n^5F_n)(t_n,x_n,v_n),\n\\]\nall quantities \\(|p_i|+|v_i-v_{i-1}|\\) and sheet parameters can be restricted to fixed bounded sets at any prescribed uniform error.</p><p>If \\(|v_n|\\to\\infty\\), the integral tends to zero. With bounded endpoint velocities, it also tends to zero if the endpoint escapes every bounded neighborhood of every dilated periodic origin. Thus positive lower bounds imply convergent positions and velocities after periodic translation and subselection.</p>", "proof": "<p>At any gain, remove its normalized tail using Lemma 7.1, and bound the other four gains by the Volterra estimate. Summing the five errors proves truncation. Large outgoing decay at the first gain proves the velocity assertion.</p><p>On retained paths, all velocities and the total flight displacement are bounded. If the transverse endpoint escapes every origin, every encountered main scale satisfies \\(\\mu\\to\\infty\\), or no core is present. Its amplitude \\(C/(t+\\mu)\\le C/\\mu\\) makes the first nonzero kernel negligible.</p><p>If the transverse coordinate is bounded relative to an origin but the axial coordinate escapes, the encountered scales satisfy \\(0&lt;\\mu\\le M\\). At bounded \\(p_3,t\\),\n\\[\n\\frac{KS(\\gamma(z-tp_3)/\\mu)}\n{\\mu+t\\alpha\\gamma S(\\gamma(z-tp_3)/\\mu)}\n\\le\\frac K\\mu S(\\gamma(z-tp_3)/\\mu)\\to0\n\\]\nuniformly in \\(0&lt;\\mu\\le M\\). For \\(|z-tp_3|\\ge Z\\), use\n\\[\n4K\\mu^{-1}e^{-2\\gamma Z/\\mu}.\n\\]\nThe dilated period \\(1/r_n\\to\\infty\\); a bounded-length path cannot visit neighborhoods of two distinct origins. Choose a nearest origin and apply these alternatives.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 7.3", "orig": "Lemma 8.4, Characteristic matching and small scales", "depth": 1, "snippet": "For the profiles \\(u_{\\mu,\\sigma}\\) of Proposition 6, uniformly on compact sets with \\(t\\ge a>0\\):", "statement": "<p>For the profiles \\(u_{\\mu,\\sigma}\\) of Proposition 6, uniformly on compact sets with \\(t\\ge a&gt;0\\):</p><p>(i) If \\(a\\le t_2\\le t_1\\), \\(\\Delta=t_1-t_2\\), and \\(z_2=z_1-\\Delta w\\), then\n\\[\n|w-u_{\\mu,\\sigma}(t_1,z_1)|\n\\le|w-u_{\\mu,\\sigma}(t_2,z_2)|.\n\\]</p><p>(ii) As \\(\\mu\\downarrow0\\),\n\\[\n\\operatorname{dist}\\bigl(u_{\\mu,\\sigma}(t,z),\n\\{z/t,\\sigma-\\alpha,\\sigma+\\alpha\\}\\bigr)\\to0.\n\\]\nFor the finite-temperature parameterization in Lemma 7.1,\n\\[\n|p_3-u_{\\mu,\\sigma}(t,z)|\\le d_s|w_3|.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>The profile satisfies\n\\[\n\\partial_zu=\\frac{(\\alpha\\gamma/\\mu)S}{1+(t\\alpha\\gamma/\\mu)S}\\ge0,\n\\qquad \\partial_tu+u\\partial_zu=0.\n\\]\nThe function\n\\[\nF(w)=w-u_{\\mu,\\sigma}(t_2,z_1-\\Delta w)\n\\]\nhas derivative at least one and root \\(u_{\\mu,\\sigma}(t_1,z_1)\\), since this profile speed is constant on its straight characteristic. Integrating \\(F'\\ge1\\) proves (i).</p><p>Fix \\(0&lt;\\delta&lt;\\alpha\\). Unless \\(u\\) is within \\(\\delta\\) of an endpoint speed,\n\\[\n\\left|\\frac{u-\\sigma}{\\alpha}\\right|\\le1-\\delta/\\alpha,\n\\]\nso\n\\[\n|z-tu|\\le\\frac\\mu\\gamma\\operatorname{arctanh}(1-\\delta/\\alpha),\n\\]\n\\[\n|u-z/t|\\le\\frac{\\mu}{\\gamma a}\n\\operatorname{arctanh}(1-\\delta/\\alpha).\n\\]\nChoose \\(\\delta\\), then \\(\\mu\\). The thermal inequality follows from the derivative-at-least-one map\n\\[\np\\mapsto p-\\sigma-\\alpha\\tanh(\\gamma(z-tp)/\\mu).\n\\]</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 7.4", "orig": "Lemma 8.5, Vanishing of two axial gains", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Fix \\(a>0\\).", "statement": "<p>Fix \\(a&gt;0\\). Suppose \\(t_n\\in[a,1]\\), endpoint positions and velocities are bounded, and \\(\\bar v_n\\to0\\). Then\n\\[\n(\\mathcal V_n^2\\mathbf1)(t_n,x_n,v_n)\\to0.\n\\]\nThe same conclusion holds when all subsequent factors in a longer finite gain integral are uniformly bounded.</p>", "proof": "<p>Remove parameter and increment tails. Retained positions and velocities are bounded, \\(t_i\\in[a,1]\\), and active scales satisfy \\(0&lt;\\mu\\le M\\); hence their largest physical width \\(d_0(r_nM)^{10}\\to0\\).</p><p>Let \\((t_1,x_1)\\), \\(x_1=(y_1,z_1)\\), be the first collision point and \\(\\xi\\) its hot input. The second axial point is\n\\[\nz_2=z_1-(t_1-t_2)\\xi_3.\n\\]\nThe first kernel has a uniform axial interval modulus \\(\\omega(\\rho)\\downarrow0\\), and for each fixed \\(b&gt;0\\), its mass on \\(|\\bar\\xi|&gt;b\\) tends to zero.</p><p>Fix an error \\(\\epsilon&gt;0\\). Choose \\(\\delta&gt;0\\) so that the first-kernel mass of the \\(\\delta\\)-neighborhoods of\n\\[\nz_1/t_1,\\quad1-\\alpha,\\quad1+\\alpha,\\quad-1-\\alpha,\\quad-1+\\alpha\n\\]\nis at most \\(\\epsilon\\). Lemma 7.3 gives \\(\\mu_0&gt;0\\) such that every \\(\\mu&lt;\\mu_0\\) profile is within \\(\\delta/4\\) of its corresponding collapsed speeds.</p><p>Only a uniformly bounded number \\(J_0\\) of mesh scales lie in \\([\\mu_0,M]\\). Choose \\(b_0&gt;0\\) so the first-kernel mass of the \\(b_0\\)-neighborhoods of\n\\[\n\\{u_{\\mu,\\sigma}(t_1,z_1):\n\\mu\\in[\\mu_0,M]\\text{ in the current mesh},\\ \\sigma=\\pm1\\}\n\\]\nis at most \\(\\epsilon\\). The list may depend on \\(n,t_1,z_1\\), but its cardinality is uniformly bounded and the interval estimate is uniform in centers.</p><p>Outside the resulting exceptional intervals \\(E_n(t_1,z_1)\\), every retained second cold speed has axial mismatch at least\n\\[\nc_*=\\frac14\\min(\\delta,b_0)&gt;0\n\\]\nfor large \\(n\\). For \\(\\mu\\ge\\mu_0\\), use characteristic matching and the thermal bound. For collapsed constant speeds, use the excluded intervals directly. For the \\(z/t\\) branch, use\n\\[\n\\xi_3-z_2/t_2=\\frac{t_1}{t_2}(\\xi_3-z_1/t_1).\n\\]</p><p>The second-kernel mismatch estimate tends to zero as \\(|\\bar\\xi|\\to0\\). Choose \\(b\\) so this mass is at most \\(\\epsilon\\) on \\(|\\bar\\xi|\\le b\\) outside \\(E_n\\). For fixed \\(b\\), the first-kernel mass on \\(|\\bar\\xi|&gt;b\\) vanishes. Its mass on \\(E_n\\) is at most \\(2\\epsilon\\), and its remaining mass is bounded. Combine the three regions and remove the initial tail error. Further gains contribute only their uniform Volterra bound.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 7.5", "orig": "Lemma 8.6, Redundant plane parameters", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Let \\(n\\ne0\\), \\(e=n/|n|\\), and let \\(\\zeta\\ge0\\) be bounded, supported in \\([-1,1]\\), with integral one.", "statement": "<p>Let \\(n\\ne0\\), \\(e=n/|n|\\), and let \\(\\zeta\\ge0\\) be bounded, supported in \\([-1,1]\\), with integral one. For every nonnegative measurable \\(F\\),\n\\[\n\\int_{n^\\perp}F(k)\\frac{dA(k)}{|n|}\n=\\int_{\\mathbb R^3}F(n\\times L)|n|\\zeta(L\\cdot e)\\,dL.\n\\]\nIf \\(\\delta\\le|n|\\le R\\) and \\(|k|\\le R_1\\), the right-side parameters satisfy\n\\[\n|L|\\le\\sqrt{(R_1/\\delta)^2+1},\n\\]\nand its density is at most \\(R\\|\\zeta\\|_\\infty\\). The identity can be applied successively when normals are measurable functions of earlier parameters.</p>", "proof": "<p>Write \\(L=L^\\perp+\\lambda e\\). The map \\(L^\\perp\\mapsto n\\times L^\\perp\\) multiplies two-dimensional area by \\(|n|^2\\), while integration in \\(\\lambda\\) contributes \\(\\int\\zeta=1\\). This proves the identity and the bounds. For successive gains, condition on all earlier parameters and use Tonelli; no derivatives of variable normals enter.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 7.6", "orig": "Lemma 8.7, Uniform pullback estimate", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Let \\(\\mathcal O\\subset\\mathbb R^k\\) be open, \\(k\\ge d\\), and \\( \\Phi_n,\\Phi:\\mathcal O\\to\\mathbb R^d,\\qquad \\Phi_n\\to\\Phi\\text{ in }C^1_{\\rm loc},\\qquad \\operatorname{rank}D\\Phi=d\\text{ almost everywhere}. \\) Let …", "statement": "<p>Let \\(\\mathcal O\\subset\\mathbb R^k\\) be open, \\(k\\ge d\\), and\n\\[\n\\Phi_n,\\Phi:\\mathcal O\\to\\mathbb R^d,\\qquad\n\\Phi_n\\to\\Phi\\text{ in }C^1_{\\rm loc},\\qquad\n\\operatorname{rank}D\\Phi=d\\text{ almost everywhere}.\n\\]\nLet \\(K\\Subset\\mathcal O\\) and \\(B\\subset\\mathbb R^d\\) be compact. Suppose measurable \\(a_n\\) are supported in \\(K\\), \\(|a_n|\\le A\\), and measurable \\(F_n\\) are supported in \\(B\\), \\(|F_n|\\le M\\), with \\(F_n\\to0\\) in measure. Then\n\\[\n\\int_{\\mathcal O}|F_n(\\Phi_n(q))a_n(q)|\\,dq\\to0.\n\\]\nNo convergence or continuity of \\(a_n\\) is required.</p>", "proof": "<p>Boundedness and convergence in measure give \\(\\|F_n\\|_1\\to0\\). Given \\(\\epsilon&gt;0\\), cover the critical set in \\(K\\) by an open \\(E\\) with \\(|E\\cap K|&lt;\\epsilon\\).</p><p>At a regular point \\(q_0\\), choose a nonzero \\(d\\)-coordinate minor. Let \\(\\pi\\) project onto complementary coordinates, and set\n\\[\nG=(\\Phi,\\pi q),\\qquad G_n=(\\Phi_n,\\pi q),\\qquad A_0=DG(q_0).\n\\]\nOn a sufficiently small convex ball \\(V\\Subset\\mathcal O\\), for all large \\(n\\),\n\\[\n\\sup_V\\|A_0^{-1}DG_n-I\\|&lt;1/2.\n\\]\nLine-segment integration proves injectivity, and the Jacobian is bounded below by \\(c_V&gt;0\\). Thus \\(G_n\\) is a diffeomorphism onto its image.</p><p>Cover \\(K\\setminus E\\) by finitely many such balls. Change variables on each:\n\\[\n\\int_{V\\cap K}|F_n(\\Phi_n(q))|\\,dq\n\\le c_V^{-1}|\\pi(V)|\\|F_n\\|_1.\n\\]\nFor \\(k=d\\), the complementary-volume factor is one. The integral over \\(E\\cap K\\), including its density, is at most \\(AM\\epsilon\\). Pass to the limit, then let \\(\\epsilon\\downarrow0\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 7.7", "orig": "Lemma 8.8, Five-gain rank and robust pullback", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Fix a nonaxial endpoint \\(v_0\\), \\((t_0,x_0)\\) with \\(t_0>0\\), positive scales \\(\\mu_i\\), and signs \\(\\sigma_i\\), \\(1\\le i\\le5\\).", "statement": "<p>Fix a nonaxial endpoint \\(v_0\\), \\((t_0,x_0)\\) with \\(t_0&gt;0\\), positive scales \\(\\mu_i\\), and signs \\(\\sigma_i\\), \\(1\\le i\\le5\\). For \\(t_i&gt;0\\), \\(L_i\\in\\mathbb R^3\\), define\n\\[\nx_i=x_{i-1}-(t_{i-1}-t_i)v_{i-1},\n\\]\n\\[\np_i=u_{\\mu_i,\\sigma_i}(t_i,z_i)e_3,\\quad\nn_i=v_{i-1}-p_i,\\quad k_i=n_i\\times L_i,\\quad\nv_i=v_{i-1}+k_i.\n\\]\nThen\n\\[\n\\Phi:(t_1,\\ldots,t_5,L_1,\\ldots,L_5)\\mapsto(t_5,x_5,v_5)\n\\]\nis real analytic on the connected open set \\((0,\\infty)^5\\times\\mathbb R^{15}\\) and has rank seven almost everywhere, in particular on the ordered region.</p><p>For convergent nonaxial endpoint sequences, positive scale sequences, and finite-temperature core formulas, the analogous maps, including bounded cold parameters \\(w_i\\), converge in \\(C^1\\) on compact parameter sets. They satisfy Lemma 7.6 for bounded measurable parameter densities and arbitrary measurable restrictions.</p>", "proof": "<p>The implicit profile has positive \\(u\\)-derivative\n\\[\n1+t\\alpha\\gamma S(\\gamma(z-tu)/\\mu)/\\mu,\n\\]\nso the implicit analytic function theorem and uniqueness give an analytic global profile. The recursion is analytic even without time ordering.</p><p>Exhibit a rank-seven point. Set all five times equal to \\(\\tau\\in(0,t_0)\\), so all collision positions coincide. Rotate about the axis and write \\(v_0=(R,0,z)\\), \\(R&gt;0\\), and \\(p_i=a_ie_3\\). Choose\n\\[\nk_1=(0,1,0),\\qquad k_2=(1,-R,0).\n\\]\nThey are perpendicular to their normals and span the transverse plane. Put\n\\[\nw=\\bar v_2=(R+1,1-R)\\ne0,\\qquad b_3=v_{2,3}-a_3,\n\\]\nand, for sufficiently small nonzero \\(\\epsilon\\), choose\n\\[\nk_3=\\epsilon\\left(-\\frac{b_3w}{|w|^2},1\\right).\n\\]\nThen \\(k_3\\perp n_3\\), its axial component is nonzero, and \\(\\bar v_3\\ne0\\). Thus \\(k_1,k_2,k_3\\) are independent. If \\(\\bar v_3=(X,Y)\\), take\n\\[\nk_4=(-Y,X,0),\\qquad k_5=0.\n\\]\nThen \\(n_4,n_5\\) are nonparallel because\n\\[\n\\det(\\bar n_4,\\bar n_5)=X^2+Y^2&gt;0.\n\\]\nRepresent each increment by \\(L_i=-n_i\\times k_i/|n_i|^2\\), and choose \\(L_5=0\\).</p><p>At equal times,\n\\[\nx_5=x_0-v_0(t_0-t_1)-\\sum_{i=1}^4v_i(t_i-t_{i+1}).\n\\]\nAll angular position derivatives vanish. Keeping \\(t_5\\) fixed,\n\\[\n\\partial_{t_1}x_5=-k_1,\\quad\n\\partial_{t_2}x_5=-k_2,\\quad\n\\partial_{t_3}x_5=-k_3.\n\\]\nDerivatives of velocities and partner profiles are multiplied by zero flight durations. Also,\n\\[\n\\delta_{L_4}v_5=n_4\\times\\delta L_4,\\qquad\n\\delta_{L_5}v_5=n_5\\times\\delta L_5.\n\\]\nTheir planes span \\(\\mathbb R^3\\). Three time columns and three angular columns form an invertible block for \\((x_5,v_5)\\); append \\(t_5\\) to obtain a nonzero seven-dimensional minor.</p><p>This minor is a nontrivial analytic function on a connected domain, so its zero set is null. The paper's argument covers that zero set by regular hypersurface charts of derivatives of one lower order at points with a first nonzero derivative. Rank failure is contained in this null set.</p><p>For finite temperature, add the fifteen \\(w_i\\)-coordinates. Positive lower scale bounds and vanishing \\(d_{r_n\\mu_i}\\) give \\(C^1\\) convergence by implicit differentiation. The limit is independent of these spectator coordinates and still has rank seven almost everywhere by Fubini. Lemma 7.6 applies.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 7.8", "orig": "Lemma 8.9, Vanishing of five nonaxial gains", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Suppose \\(\\ell_n\\to0\\), cap endpoints converge after periodic translation with nonaxial limiting velocity, and \\( 0\\le f_n\\le2\\quad(a\\le t\\le\\tau_n), \\) …", "statement": "<p>Suppose \\(\\ell_n\\to0\\), cap endpoints converge after periodic translation with nonaxial limiting velocity, and\n\\[\n0\\le f_n\\le2\\quad(a\\le t\\le\\tau_n),\n\\]\n\\[\nf_n\\to0\\text{ in measure on compact subsets of }\n(0,U)\\times\\mathbb R_x^3\\times\\{\\bar v\\ne0\\}.\n\\]\nThen\n\\[\n(\\mathcal V_n^5f_n)(\\tau_n,x_n,v_n)\\to0.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Remove tails by Lemma 7.2. All path positions and velocities are bounded; every main core has \\(s\\le Cr_n\\), so transverse widths vanish uniformly.</p><p>Starting from the positive endpoint transverse-speed lower bound, apply Lemma 1.4's nonaxial small-input estimate successively at gains one through five. Choose thresholds \\(b_i&gt;0\\) so deleting \\(|\\bar v_i|&lt;b_i\\) has arbitrarily small total cost, using uniform bounds for later gains. All retained transverse speeds are then bounded below by a fixed \\(b&gt;0\\), and all normals are at least \\(b/2\\).</p><p>Delete collision points with \\(|y_i|&lt;\\rho\\). Conditional on earlier parameters, \\(y_i\\) is affine in \\(t_i\\), with derivative \\(\\bar v_{i-1}\\), so\n\\[\n|\\{t_i:|y_i|&lt;\\rho\\}|\\le2\\rho/b.\n\\]\nThe kernel mass bounds make the total deletion at most \\(C_a\\rho/b\\). Retained scales now lie in a compact positive interval. Mesh convergence leaves only finitely many five-tuples of scales and signs.</p><p>If \\(U&gt;1\\), the target time \\(t_5\\in[a,1]\\) is interior. If \\(U=1\\), delete \\(t_5&gt;1-\\delta\\). Ordering puts all five times into an interval of length at most \\(\\delta\\), at cost\n\\[\n2(C/a)^5\\delta^5/5!.\n\\]</p><p>For one retained tuple, construct maps with masks removed and retain all masks in the integrand. Lemma 7.5 changes the five plane integrals into integration over five times, fifteen \\(L\\)-coordinates, and fifteen cold parameters. The support is a fixed compact subset of \\((0,\\infty)^5\\times\\mathbb R^{30}\\). Densities are bounded because amplitudes, normals, and retained ratios \\(H(v_i)/H(v_{i-1})\\) are bounded; masks and ordering are measurable factors in \\([0,1]\\).</p><p>The actual targets lie in a compact set \\(B\\) inside the weak-limit region and away from the axis. Set\n\\[\nF_n=f_n\\mathbf1_B\\mathbf1_{\\{a\\le t\\le\\tau_n\\}},\n\\]\nextended by zero. It is bounded and tends to zero in measure. Lemmas 7.7 and 7.6 make this tuple's integral vanish. Sum the finitely many tuples and let all deletion tolerances tend to zero.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 8", "orig": "Proposition 9.1, Branching growth and the dilated horizon", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p>Consider the backgrounds \\(B\\), limits \\(g\\), lower endpoints \\(l\\in[0,1]\\), and geometric meshes of Proposition 6, with nonvanishing from Proposition 7. The construction parameters \\(\\eta,\\alpha,\\gamma,K\\) have not yet received their final fixed values.</p><p>For incident velocities \\(v,w\\) and \\(n\\in S^2\\), define\n\\[\n\\Phi(v,w,n)=\\frac{v+w}{2}+\\frac{|v-w|}{2}n,\\qquad d\\varsigma(n)=dn/(4\\pi).\n\\]\nA collision has rate \\(2\\pi|v-w|\\), and either child's marginal is the pushforward of \\(\\varsigma\\) under \\(\\Phi\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There are fixed positive choices of \\(\\eta,\\alpha,\\gamma,K\\), with \\(\\eta,\\alpha,\\gamma\\) small and \\(K\\) large, such that every limit in Propositions 6–7 has\n\\[\nU&lt;\\infty.\n\\]\nThese choices are uniform in mesh phase and \\(l\\), and precede the final choices of \\(s_0,d_0,T_0\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Suppose an extracted limit has \\(U=\\infty\\). Proposition 7 gives \\(g\\not\\equiv0\\). Lemma 8.8 supplies a sufficiently late \\(t_0\\) with mass \\(m_0&gt;0\\) in \\(\\mathcal B_{t_0}\\), and all later blocks lie outside the hole.</p><p>For a start in \\(\\mathcal B_t\\), apply Lemma 8.6 with Lemma 8.7's simultaneous scaling. Returning to physical dilated coordinates, selected histories satisfy\n\\[\n|x_{\\rm end}-x_{\\rm start}-\\kappa\\Delta e_1|&lt;d\\kappa\\Delta.\n\\]\nThe starting radius \\(d\\kappa t\\) places endpoints in \\(\\mathcal B_{t+\\Delta}\\), with final velocities in \\(E\\). History weights are unchanged: the scaled gain rate is divided by \\(\\rho_0\\), time differentials are multiplied by \\(\\rho_0\\), and integrated loss is invariant.</p><p>Integrate the kernel of all histories with \\(1\\le j\\le J\\) satisfying the block restrictions. Its weight exceeds three uniformly over starts, certified by the finite tests without a measurable choice of individual tests. Lemma 8.1 gives, at \\(t_n=(1+a)^nt_0\\),\n\\[\n\\int_{\\mathcal B_{t_n}}g(t_n)\\ge3^nm_0.\n\\]\nBut the cap and \\(l\\le1\\) give\n\\[\n\\int_{\\mathcal B_{t_n}}g(t_n)\n\\le(t_n+1)\\frac{4\\pi}{3}(d\\kappa t_n)^3\\int_EH(v)\\,dv\n\\le C(1+t_n)^4.\n\\]\nThis contradicts \\((1+a)^4&lt;3\\). The starting mass and time may depend on the limit; the block parameters do not. Hence every extracted horizon is finite.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 8.1", "orig": "Lemma 9.2, Positive histories and their normalization", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Let \\( \\widetilde B(t,x)=\\sum_{\\sigma=\\pm1}b_\\sigma(t,x)\\delta_{w_\\sigma(t,x)}, \\) with Borel representatives, and set \\( q_\\sigma(t,x,v)=2\\pi b_\\sigma(t,x)|v-w_\\sigma(t,x)|,\\qquad \\nu=\\sum_\\sigma q_\\sigma. \\) Suppose …", "statement": "<p>Let\n\\[\n\\widetilde B(t,x)=\\sum_{\\sigma=\\pm1}b_\\sigma(t,x)\\delta_{w_\\sigma(t,x)},\n\\]\nwith Borel representatives, and set\n\\[\nq_\\sigma(t,x,v)=2\\pi b_\\sigma(t,x)|v-w_\\sigma(t,x)|,\\qquad\n\\nu=\\sum_\\sigma q_\\sigma.\n\\]\nSuppose \\(f\\ge0\\) solves\n\\[\nD_tf=2P(\\widetilde B,f)-\\nu f\n\\]\nin the local transport sense, with locally integrable coefficients and collision terms on the bounded positive-time regions considered.</p><p>A history starting at time \\(a\\), with ordered collisions \\(a&lt;\\tau_1&lt;\\cdots&lt;\\tau_j&lt;b\\), signs \\(\\sigma_i\\), and directions \\(n_i\\), follows free flights and\n\\[\nV_{\\tau_i}=\\Phi(V_{\\tau_i-},w_{\\sigma_i}(\\tau_i,X_{\\tau_i}),n_i).\n\\]\nIts weight relative to \\(\\prod_i d\\tau_i\\,d\\varsigma(n_i)\\) is\n\\[\n\\exp\\left[-\\int_a^b\\nu(s,X_s,V_s)\\,ds\\right]\n\\prod_{i=1}^j2q_{\\sigma_i}(\\tau_i,X_{\\tau_i},V_{\\tau_i-}).\n\\]\nFor any nonnegative portion of \\(f(a)\\), integrating any collection of bounded finite histories produces a measure dominated by \\(f(b)\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Lemma 1.1 gives\n\\[\n\\int P(B,f)(v)\\,dv=\\int\\nu_B(v)f(v)\\,dv.\n\\]\nThus the coefficient two counts both children and loss removes their parent once.</p><p>In free coordinates, \\(f^\\sharp(t,x,v)=f(t,x+tv,v)\\) satisfies\n\\[\n\\partial_tf^\\sharp=(2P(\\widetilde B,f)-\\nu f)^\\sharp.\n\\]\nLocal integrability gives absolutely continuous sections and time traces. The scalar integrating factor expresses free survival plus the nonnegative gain integral. Repeated substitution produces the stated history weights; every remainder is nonnegative. Tonelli justifies the substitutions and restrictions. With total gain rate bounded by \\(C\\), the \\(j\\)-collision term is bounded by \\(C^j(b-a)^j/j!\\) times initial mass.</p><p>The formulas also apply to point-velocity partners, whose gain is a density by Lemma 1.1. Starting with a density, free flight and gain preserve absolute continuity. Null spatial changes of representatives do not alter the integrated formulas.</p><p>For the actual background,\n\\[\n|u_\\sigma|\\le1+\\alpha,\\qquad\n0\\le b_\\sigma\\le\\frac{\\theta K}{t\\alpha\\gamma},\\qquad\n\\nu_B(t,x,v)\\le\\frac{C(1+|v|)}t.\n\\]\nChoose outer-annulus values at interfaces and zero on the axis. Bounded finite flights beginning at positive time have strictly positive survival. Proposition 6 supplies the required traces and integrability, with the cap at those traces.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 8.2", "orig": "Lemma 9.3, Reference flight with a positive potential", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Set \\( D=5,\\qquad E=\\{|v|\\le D\\},\\qquad B_0=\\delta_{e_3}+\\delta_{-e_3}, \\) \\( \\nu_0(v)=2\\pi(|v-e_3|+|v+e_3|). \\) Let \\(J(v,dv')\\) choose a sign with probability proportional to \\(|v-\\sigma e_3|\\), then choose …", "statement": "<p>Set\n\\[\nD=5,\\qquad E=\\{|v|\\le D\\},\\qquad B_0=\\delta_{e_3}+\\delta_{-e_3},\n\\]\n\\[\n\\nu_0(v)=2\\pi(|v-e_3|+|v+e_3|).\n\\]\nLet \\(J(v,dv')\\) choose a sign with probability proportional to \\(|v-\\sigma e_3|\\), then choose \\(\\Phi(v,\\sigma e_3,n)\\) with \\(n\\sim\\varsigma\\). Put \\(p(v)=J(v,E)\\).</p><p>For \\(v\\in E\\),\n\\[\np(v)\\ge25/26.\n\\]\nThe reference branching history sum restricted to velocities in \\(E\\) is represented by a Markov flight with jump rate and law\n\\[\n\\lambda(v)=2p(v)\\nu_0(v),\\qquad\nJ(v,dv')\\mathbf1_E(v')/p(v),\n\\]\nand weight\n\\[\n\\exp\\left(\\int_0^A\\mathcal W(V_s)\\,ds\\right),\\qquad\n\\mathcal W(v)=(2p(v)-1)\\nu_0(v).\n\\]\nOne may take\n\\[\n0&lt;c_0=48\\pi/13\\le\\mathcal W(v),\\qquad\n\\lambda(v)\\le C_0=48\\pi.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Set \\(s=|v|^2\\), \\(z=v_3\\), and\n\\[\nR=\\sqrt{(s+1)^2-4z^2}.\n\\]\nFor either partner sign, the squared child speed has law\n\\[\n|v'|^2=\\frac{s+1+RZ}{2},\\qquad Z\\text{ uniform on }[-1,1].\n\\]\nThis follows from the uniform coordinate law on \\(S^2\\). The maximum is at most \\(s+1\\), so there is no exit for \\(s\\le24\\). If \\(24&lt;s\\le25\\), a positive exit probability is\n\\[\n\\frac12+\\frac{s-49}{2R}\n\\le\\frac12+\\frac{s-49}{2(s+1)}\n=\\frac{s-24}{s+1}\\le\\frac1{26}.\n\\]\nFor \\(R=0\\), the deterministic squared speed is at most \\(25\\).</p><p>A Markov history has survival \\(e^{-\\int\\lambda}\\). Multiplication by \\(e^{\\int(\\lambda-\\nu_0)}\\) changes its factors exactly into the restricted branching weights. Both finite-time history sums converge absolutely because rates are bounded. Finally,\n\\[\n4\\pi\\le\\nu_0(v)\\le24\\pi,\\qquad2p(v)-1\\ge12/13,\n\\]\ngiving the constants.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 8.3", "orig": "Lemma 9.4, Three collisions give a volume minorization", "depth": 1, "snippet": "For the reference velocity process in Lemma 8.2, let \\(P_t(v,\\cdot)\\) be its transition law.", "statement": "<p>For the reference velocity process in Lemma 8.2, let \\(P_t(v,\\cdot)\\) be its transition law. There exist \\(r_0,\\varepsilon_0&gt;0\\) such that\n\\[\nP_1(v,A)\\ge\\varepsilon_0|A\\cap B(0,r_0)|\\qquad(v\\in E)\n\\]\nfor every Borel \\(A\\subset E\\). For every \\(\\zeta&gt;0\\), there is \\(\\varepsilon_\\zeta&gt;0\\) with\n\\[\nP_1(v,B(e_1,\\zeta)\\cap\\{|v'|&lt;D\\})\\ge\\varepsilon_\\zeta.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Choose a sign with \\(|v-\\sigma e_3|\\ge1\\). Use it first and choose an output within a fixed small \\(\\delta\\) of \\(\\sigma e_3\\). The incident distance lies in \\([1,6]\\), so this cap has a uniform positive probability and lies strictly in \\(E\\).</p><p>Use partner \\(-\\sigma e_3\\) next and restrict its output \\(u\\) near \\(e_1\\). When the first output is exactly \\(\\sigma e_3\\), this sphere is the unit sphere. For the third collision use \\(e_3\\), giving\n\\[\nT(u,n)=\\frac{u+e_3}{2}+\\frac{|u-e_3|}{2}n.\n\\]\nAt \\(u=e_1\\), \\(n_0=-(e_1+e_3)/\\sqrt2\\), the output is zero. The two \\(n\\)-derivatives span \\((e_1+e_3)^\\perp\\), while\n\\[\n\\partial_{e_3}T(e_1,n_0)=\\frac{e_1+3e_3}{4},\\qquad\n(e_1+e_3)\\cdot\\partial_{e_3}T=1.\n\\]\nThe last-two-collision angular map has rank three.</p><p>Use the two \\(n\\)-coordinates and one \\(u\\)-coordinate as distinguished variables, holding the fourth angular coordinate in a short interval. The inverse function theorem, with the first output as parameter, provides uniform neighborhoods, bounded Jacobians and inverse Jacobians, and an image containing a fixed ball. Shrink neighborhoods and \\(\\delta\\) for both signs. Sphere area has positive lower coordinate density, so change of variables gives a fixed multiple of volume.</p><p>Put the three times into fixed disjoint subintervals of \\((0,1)\\) and prohibit other jumps. Selected relative speeds are bounded below, and the selected Markov transition intensity is\n\\[\n4\\pi|v-\\sigma e_3|\n\\mathbf1_E(\\Phi(v,\\sigma e_3,n))\\,d\\varsigma(n).\n\\]\nThe no-additional-jump factor is at least \\(e^{-C_0}\\). This proves minorization. Keeping only the first two collisions proves access to a patch near \\(e_1\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 8.4", "orig": "Lemma 9.5, Mixing and displacement", "depth": 1, "snippet": "The reference process has a stationary probability \\(\\pi\\) with \\( \\int v\\,d\\pi(v)=0. \\) There exist \\(C,c>0\\) such that \\( \\sup_{v\\in E}\\|P_t(v,\\cdot)-\\pi\\|_{\\rm TV}\\le Ce^{-ct}, \\) …", "statement": "<p>The reference process has a stationary probability \\(\\pi\\) with\n\\[\n\\int v\\,d\\pi(v)=0.\n\\]\nThere exist \\(C,c&gt;0\\) such that\n\\[\n\\sup_{v\\in E}\\|P_t(v,\\cdot)-\\pi\\|_{\\rm TV}\\le Ce^{-ct},\n\\]\n\\[\n\\sup_{v\\in E}\\mathbb E_v\\left|A^{-1}\\int_0^AV_s\\,ds\\right|^2\n\\le C/A\\qquad(A\\ge1).\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Normalize the common minorization measure in Lemma 8.3. It has positive mass \\(\\epsilon\\) in every row of \\(P_1\\); removing it makes \\(\\mu\\mapsto\\mu P_1\\) contract total variation by \\(1-\\epsilon\\). Completeness gives a unique stationary probability and uniform geometric convergence. Since \\(P_t\\) commutes with \\(P_1\\), \\(\\pi P_t=\\pi\\); contractivity fills the times between integers.</p><p>Velocity negation interchanges partner signs and preserves \\(E\\), so uniqueness makes \\(\\pi\\) symmetric. Thus its mean is zero and\n\\[\n\\sup_v|\\mathbb E_vV_t|\\le Ce^{-ct}.\n\\]\nThe Markov property and bounded velocities imply\n\\[\n|\\mathbb E(V_s\\cdot V_t)|\\le Ce^{-c(t-s)}\\qquad(s\\le t).\n\\]\nDouble integration bounds the second moment of \\(\\int_0^AV_s\\,ds\\) by \\(CA\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 8.5", "orig": "Lemma 9.6, A displacement block", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Fix \\(d=1/10\\) and \\( 0<\\kappa<\\min\\left\\{\\frac14,\\frac{c_0}{2C_0},\\frac{r_0}{4}\\right\\}. \\) There is a finite \\(L\\) such that, for \\( A\\in I_L=\\left[\\frac L{1+d},\\frac L{1-d}\\right],\\qquad v\\in E, \\) reference …", "statement": "<p>Fix \\(d=1/10\\) and\n\\[\n0&lt;\\kappa&lt;\\min\\left\\{\\frac14,\\frac{c_0}{2C_0},\\frac{r_0}{4}\\right\\}.\n\\]\nThere is a finite \\(L\\) such that, for\n\\[\nA\\in I_L=\\left[\\frac L{1+d},\\frac L{1-d}\\right],\\qquad v\\in E,\n\\]\nreference branching histories remaining in \\(E\\), ending strictly inside \\(E\\), and having displacement \\(Z_A\\) with\n\\[\n|Z_A-\\kappa Ae_1|&lt;\\frac34d\\kappa A\n\\]\nhave total weight greater than eight.</p>", "proof": "<p>Put \\(A_0=(1-\\kappa)A-1\\). On the first interval of length \\(A_0\\), require integrated velocity of norm at most \\(Ad\\kappa/4\\). Lemma 8.4 and Chebyshev give probability at least \\(1/2\\), uniformly for sufficiently large \\(A\\). On the next unit interval, require final velocity in \\(B(e_1,d/4)\\), with conditional probability at least a fixed \\(\\epsilon&gt;0\\) by Lemma 8.3. Require no jump in the last interval of length \\(\\kappa A\\), with conditional probability at least \\(e^{-C_0\\kappa A}\\).</p><p>The displacement errors are at most \\(Ad\\kappa/4\\), \\(D\\), and \\(Ad\\kappa/4\\). For \\(A&gt;4D/(d\\kappa)\\), their sum is less than \\(3Ad\\kappa/4\\). The final velocity is interior. Lemma 8.2 gives branching weight at least\n\\[\n\\frac\\epsilon2e^{(c_0-C_0\\kappa)A}.\n\\]\nThe exponent is positive. Choose \\(L\\) so this exceeds eight and all required lower bounds hold throughout \\(I_L\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 8.6", "orig": "Lemma 9.7, Measurable perturbations", "depth": 1, "snippet": "For the fixed \\(\\kappa,L\\) in Lemma 8.5, there exist \\(J\\in\\mathbb N\\), \\(\\epsilon_*\\in(0,1/4)\\) as follows.", "statement": "<p>For the fixed \\(\\kappa,L\\) in Lemma 8.5, there exist \\(J\\in\\mathbb N\\), \\(\\epsilon_*\\in(0,1/4)\\) as follows. Suppose a measurable medium satisfies, throughout every speed-\\(D\\) excursion from a specified point over duration \\(A\\in I_L\\),\n\\[\n|b_\\sigma-1|\\le\\epsilon_*,\\qquad\n|w_\\sigma-\\sigma e_3|\\le\\epsilon_*\\qquad(\\sigma=\\pm1).\n\\]\nFrom every starting velocity in \\(E\\), histories with at most \\(J\\) collisions, speed at most \\(D\\) on every flight, all postcollision and final velocities strictly inside \\(E\\), and\n\\[\n|Z_A-\\kappa Ae_1|&lt;d\\kappa A\n\\]\nhave total weight greater than three. The initial flight may have speed \\(D\\); no continuity of the medium is needed.</p>", "proof": "<p>For a fixed reference start \\((v,A)\\in E\\times I_L\\), discard the zero-collision term, whose weight is at most one, from Lemma 8.5's family. Weight greater than seven remains. Monotone convergence restricts to finitely many positive counts. Postcollision velocities are interior almost surely: a nondegenerate diameter sphere meets \\(\\partial E\\) in sphere-area measure zero, and degenerate collisions have zero rate.</p><p>Using strict displacement and velocity restrictions, separated ordered times, sphere charts, and inner regularity, retain compact parameter tests with weight greater than six and positive margins. Parameterize times by fractions of \\(A\\). Reference paths and weights are continuous in parameters and in \\((v,A)\\), so these tests retain weight greater than five nearby. Compactness of \\(E\\times I_L\\) gives finitely many tests, a maximum count \\(J\\), and uniform margins.</p><p>Use the same initial velocity, signs, directions, and fractional times for perturbed paths. Since\n\\[\n|\\Phi(v,w,n)-\\Phi(\\widetilde v,\\widetilde w,n)|\n\\le|v-\\widetilde v|+|w-\\widetilde w|,\n\\]\nvelocity errors after \\(j\\) collisions are at most \\(j\\epsilon_*\\), and displacement error at most \\(AJ\\epsilon_*\\). No medium comparison at nearby positions is used: its value at the actual position is close to the constant reference partner. Small \\(\\epsilon_*\\) preserves all margins and thus the speed bound, by induction up to a possible first failure.</p><p>Relative-speed and full loss-rate errors are \\(O_J(\\epsilon_*)\\), as are gain-factor errors. Bounded durations and finite products give uniform history-weight errors \\(O_{J,L}(\\epsilon_*)\\). The finite tests have finite parameter measure. Decrease \\(\\epsilon_*\\) until their weights exceed three.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 8.7", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Uniform parameter choice, original Section 9.5.", "statement": "<p>Uniform parameter choice, original Section 9.5.</p><p>The construction parameters can be fixed independently of \\(l\\), mesh phase, and the limit so that Lemma 8.6 applies to every sufficiently late block beginning in\n\\[\n\\mathcal B_t=\\{(x,v):|x-\\kappa te_1|\\le d\\kappa t,\\ |v|\\le D\\}.\n\\]\nSpecifically, there are fixed \\(\\eta,\\alpha,\\gamma,K&gt;0\\) and\n\\[\n\\Delta=\\frac{L\\kappa t}{\\theta K},\\qquad a=\\frac{L\\kappa}{\\theta K},\n\\]\nsuch that \\((1+a)^4&lt;3\\), and each starting point in \\(\\mathcal B_t\\), after simultaneous time-space scaling by\n\\[\n\\rho_0=\\theta K/|y_0|,\n\\]\nhas duration\n\\[\nA=\\rho_0\\Delta=\\frac{L\\kappa t}{|y_0|}\\in I_L\n\\]\nand medium satisfying Lemma 8.6's closeness assumptions on all speed-\\(D\\) excursions.</p>", "proof": "<p>First reserve \\(\\alpha_{\\rm acc}&gt;0\\), depending only on \\(\\kappa\\) and Lemma 8.3, so that after a first output sufficiently close to \\(\\sigma e_3\\), two more collisions with partners within \\(\\alpha_{\\rm acc}\\) of \\(-\\sigma e_3,e_3\\) have positive angular probability of ending in\n\\[\nB(\\kappa e_1,d\\kappa/4).\n\\]\nThe inverse-coordinate construction of Lemma 8.3 maps a positive-measure set into \\(B(\\kappa e_1,d\\kappa/8)\\subset B(0,r_0)\\). Retain a compact positive-measure subset and use uniform continuity of the two sphere maps. Take the smaller tolerance for both signs and less than half the permitted first-output radius.</p><p>The constants \\(\\kappa,L,J,\\epsilon_*,\\alpha_{\\rm acc}\\) are now fixed. At \\(x_0\\in\\mathcal B_t\\),\n\\[\n(1-d)\\kappa t\\le|y_0|\\le(1+d)\\kappa t.\n\\]\nA speed-\\(D\\) excursion over \\([t,t+\\Delta]\\) satisfies\n\\[\n\\bigl||y|-|y_0|\\bigr|\\le D\\Delta,\\qquad\n|z|\\le d\\kappa t+D\\Delta.\n\\]\nSet\n\\[\n\\delta_K=\\frac{DL}{\\theta K(1-d)}.\n\\]\nIncrease \\(K\\) until \\(\\delta_K\\le1/4\\), \\(a\\le1/4\\). For sufficiently large \\(t\\), every excursion is outside the hole, since \\(l\\le1\\). Its active scale obeys\n\\[\ne^{-\\eta}|y|\\le\\mu\\le|y|,\n\\]\nand, with fixed \\(C_1\\),\n\\[\n\\left|\\frac{|y_0|}{\\mu}-1\\right|\\le C_1(\\eta+\\delta_K),\n\\quad \\frac{\\tau}{\\mu}\\le C_1/\\kappa,\n\\quad \\frac{|z-\\tau u_\\sigma|}{\\mu}\\le C_1/\\kappa.\n\\]\nThe scaled density ratio is\n\\[\n\\frac{b_\\sigma(\\tau,x)}{\\rho_0}\n=\\frac{|y_0|}{\\mu}\n\\frac{S_\\sigma}{1+(\\tau\\alpha\\gamma/\\mu)S_\\sigma},\n\\qquad S_\\sigma=S(\\gamma(z-\\tau u_\\sigma)/\\mu).\n\\]\nUsing \\(1-\\operatorname{sech}^2h=\\tanh^2h\\le h^2\\),\n\\[\n\\left|\\frac{b_\\sigma}{\\rho_0}-1\\right|\n\\le C_2\\left(\\eta+\\delta_K+(\\gamma/\\kappa)^2+\\alpha\\gamma/\\kappa\\right),\n\\qquad |u_\\sigma-\\sigma|\\le\\alpha.\n\\]</p><p>Choose \\(\\eta&gt;0\\) small, then\n\\[\n0&lt;\\alpha&lt;\\min\\{1/4,\\epsilon_*,\\alpha_{\\rm acc}\\},\n\\]\nthen \\(\\gamma&gt;0\\) small so the non-\\(\\delta_K\\) terms total less than \\(\\epsilon_*/2\\). Finally increase \\(K\\) so the remaining term is below \\(\\epsilon_*/2\\), all preliminary restrictions hold, and \\((1+a)^4&lt;3\\).</p><p>These choices precede the final small \\(s_0,d_0,T_0\\). Small \\(\\alpha\\gamma\\) can enlarge earlier fixed constants, but introduces no approximation-index dependence.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 8.8", "orig": "Lemma 9.8, Access to a late box", "depth": 1, "snippet": "For the fixed parameters of Lemma 8.7, let \\(g\\not\\equiv0\\) solve \\( D_tg=2P(B,g)-g\\nu_B\\quad(0<t<\\infty), \\qquad0\\le g\\le(t+l)H, \\) with any allowed mesh and \\(l\\in[0,1]\\).", "statement": "<p>For the fixed parameters of Lemma 8.7, let \\(g\\not\\equiv0\\) solve\n\\[\nD_tg=2P(B,g)-g\\nu_B\\quad(0&lt;t&lt;\\infty),\n\\qquad0\\le g\\le(t+l)H,\n\\]\nwith any allowed mesh and \\(l\\in[0,1]\\). Then\n\\[\n\\int_{\\mathcal B_t}g(t,x,v)\\,dx\\,dv&gt;0\n\\]\nat arbitrarily large finite times.</p>", "proof": "<p>At some \\(a_0&gt;0\\), a bounded portion of \\(g(a_0)\\) has positive mass. Since the velocity axis is null, restrict to\n\\[\n|x|\\le R,\\qquad |v|\\le R,\\qquad|\\bar v|\\ge\\delta&gt;0.\n\\]\nA sufficiently long finite free flight gives\n\\[\n|y+s\\bar v|\\ge s\\delta-R&gt;l+2.\n\\]\nIts survival is uniformly positive by the bound \\(\\nu_B\\le C(1+|v|)/t\\). Lemma 8.1 gives positive mass outside the hole at \\(a_1=a_0+s\\).</p><p>Choose \\(h&gt;0\\) so paths with initial speed at most \\(R\\) and later speeds at most three stay in a bounded region with \\(|y|&gt;l+1\\) during \\([a_1,a_1+h]\\). Both beam densities have positive lower bounds there: time, axial position, and transverse radius are bounded, the radius is bounded away from zero, and \\(e^{-\\eta}|y|\\le\\mu\\le|y|\\). Selected rates have finite upper bounds.</p><p>Force three collisions in disjoint subintervals. Choose a first reference sign with \\(|v-\\sigma e_3|\\ge1\\), giving actual relative speed at least \\(1-\\alpha\\ge3/4\\). Choose a small cap about the actual partner, with positive uniformly bounded-below probability for bounded inputs. It lies in the reserved first-output neighborhood. Use signs \\(-\\sigma,+1\\) next and the compact angular tests defining \\(\\alpha_{\\rm acc}\\). The final velocity lies in \\(B(\\kappa e_1,d\\kappa/4)\\); intermediate speeds are at most three and selected relative speeds are bounded below. Gain, angular, time, and survival factors give positive weight, even for measurable coefficients.</p><p>Thus at \\(a_2=a_1+h\\) there is a bounded positive portion with \\(|v-\\kappa e_1|&lt;d\\kappa/4\\). If \\(R_2\\) bounds \\(|x-\\kappa a_2e_1|\\), then for every sufficiently large finite \\(t\\),\n\\[\n|x+(t-a_2)v-\\kappa te_1|\n\\le R_2+\\frac{d\\kappa}{4}(t-a_2)&lt;d\\kappa t.\n\\]\nIts speed is below \\(D\\), and each finite free-flight survival is positive. Lemma 8.1's zero-collision term gives the claim.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 9", "orig": "Lemma 10.1, Dormant dilation", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p>Fix all construction parameters as in Proposition 8 and then choose the final \\(s_0,d_0,T_0\\) to satisfy the preceding estimates. Let \\(F^d\\) be any dormant fixed-horizon limit on \\([0,T_0]\\) from Proposition 5. Evaluate spatial arguments in the lift centered at the origin.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>As \\(T\\downarrow0\\),\n\\[\nT^{-1}F^d(Tt,Tx,v)\\longrightarrow0\n\\]\nin distributions on compact subsets of\n\\[\n\\{0&lt;t&lt;1,\\ x\\in\\mathbb R^3,\\ v\\in\\mathbb R^3,\\ \\bar v\\ne0\\}.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>The dormant majorant passes to the weak limit. Its hot part is at most \\(t_{\\rm old}^2H\\), so after dilation it is bounded by \\(Tt^2H\\), vanishing locally in \\(L^1\\).</p><p>At a bounded dilated transverse position \\(y\\), any main annulus at \\(y_{\\rm old}=Ty\\) has \\(s\\le CT\\). For sufficiently small \\(T\\), the relevant copy is centered at the origin. On \\(|\\bar v|\\ge\\varepsilon&gt;0\\), the cold Gaussian gives\n\\[\nc_{\\rm main}(Tt,Tx,v)\n\\le Cs^{-31}\\exp\\left(-\\frac{c\\varepsilon^2}{d_0^2s^{20}}\\right).\n\\]\nMaximized over \\(0&lt;s\\le CT\\), this tends to zero faster than any power of \\(T\\). At points with no main color only escaped tails occur. Their bound\n\\[\nC_A(Tt)^Ae^{-4|v|^2}\n\\]\nvanishes after multiplication by \\(T^{-1}\\) for \\(A&gt;1\\). The estimates are uniform on each indicated compact set.</p>"}], "edges": [[1, 11, "cite"], [1, 13, "cite"], [1, 16, "cite"], [1, 18, "cite"], [1, 28, "cite"], [1, 31, "cite"], [2, 1, "cite"], [2, 40, "cite"], [3, 1, "cite"], [3, 4, "cite"], [3, 5, "cite"], [3, 7, "cite"], [4, 1, "cite"], [4, 5, "cite"], [4, 6, "cite"], [5, 1, "cite"], [5, 15, "cite"], [5, 31, "cite"], [5, 38, "cite"], [6, 1, "cite"], [6, 15, "cite"], [7, 1, "cite"], [8, 0, "cite"], [8, 11, "cite"], [8, 18, "cite"], [8, 19, "cite"], [8, 20, "cite"], [9, 8, "cite"], [10, 8, "cite"], [11, 18, "cite"], [11, 21, "cite"], [11, 25, "cite"], [11, 27, "cite"], [11, 28, "cite"], [11, 30, "cite"], [11, 31, "cite"], [12, 11, "cite"], [12, 13, "cite"], [13, 11, "cite"], [14, 11, "cite"], [14, 16, "cite"], [15, 11, "cite"], [15, 16, "cite"], [16, 11, "cite"], [17, 11, "cite"], [18, 0, "cite"], [18, 19, "cite"], [19, 0, "cite"], [19, 26, "cite"], [19, 48, "cite"], [20, 19, "cite"], [20, 24, "cite"], [21, 19, "cite"], [21, 23, "cite"], [21, 24, "cite"], [22, 19, "cite"], [22, 23, "cite"], [22, 28, "cite"], [23, 19, "cite"], [23, 24, "cite"], [24, 19, "cite"], [24, 25, "cite"], [25, 19, "cite"], [26, 0, "cite"], [26, 30, "cite"], [26, 33, "cite"], [26, 39, "cite"], [26, 40, "cite"], [27, 26, "cite"], [27, 28, "cite"], [28, 26, "cite"], [29, 26, "cite"], [30, 0, "cite"], [30, 39, "cite"], [31, 30, "cite"], [31, 32, "cite"], [31, 33, "cite"], [32, 30, "cite"], [32, 38, "cite"], [33, 34, "cite"], [34, 30, "cite"], [35, 38, "cite"], [36, 37, "cite"], [36, 38, "cite"], [37, 38, "cite"], [38, 30, "cite"], [39, 0, "cite"], [39, 48, "cite"], [40, 39, "cite"], [40, 47, "cite"], [41, 42, "cite"], [41, 44, "cite"], [42, 43, "cite"], [42, 44, "cite"], [42, 46, "cite"], [43, 44, "cite"], [44, 45, "cite"], [45, 39, "cite"], [45, 46, "cite"], [46, 39, "cite"], [46, 47, "cite"], [47, 39, "cite"], [48, 0, "cite"]]});
